@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix pmw:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/works/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia — chaos magic: Carroll, The Apophenion
#
# The harvest of one work of the corpus of pmt:ChaosMagic, kept apart from
# traditions/chaos-magic.ttl, which declares the tradition and the work
# (pmw:TheApophenion), carries its reading notes and harvest counts, and
# holds the seven nodes entered from chapter 5, part 3 on 2026-10-22, which
# are not repeated here and are linked where a node below depends on them.
# Harvested complete under prima-materia#893 with two independent extraction
# passes per part, a comparison of the two by offset and word overlap, and a
# check at the text of every item the second pass found and the first did
# not. Part 1: front matter and chapters 1-4; part 2: chapters 5-8,
# appendices I-V, epilogue and notes. Edition: Mandrake of Oxford, 2008,
# first edition (08/08/08 on the copyright page). Cited by chapter, part and
# printed page; pages whose number is missing from the layer are given only
# where they lie between two printed ones. Signs of success and conditions
# of failure carry a note beginning "Counted as a sign of success:" or
# "Counted as a condition of failure:".
#


#
# Front matter, Acknowledgements
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPhysicistsAssessment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Physicists' assessment of the book's hypotheses"@en, "Einschätzung der Hypothesen durch Physiker"@de ;
    skos:definition "Several unnamed physicists consulted on the book's hypotheses gave criticism or references; some worried about the hypothesis's high degree of symmetry, most thought it needed more mathematics to be fully justified, none found obvious holes, though it looked like a long shot."@en ;
    skos:note "Statement on how tested the book's cosmological model is."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), front matter, Acknowledgements, p. 5; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Chapter 1, Apophenia - Introduction
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMagicPhysicsContinuum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic and physics as one continuum"@en, "Magie und Physik als Kontinuum"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since magic works at least sometimes, it must belong to any full account of how the world operates; physics is treated as the fairly dependable portion of magic, and magic as a physics still to be understood and made more reliable. An extended physics should in turn suggest refinements of magical theory and practice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, p. 7; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophParadigmCrash a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Paradigm crash: Relativity, Quantum physics and Chaoism converge"@en, "Paradigmenzusammenbruch: Relativität, Quantenphysik und Chaoismus konvergieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "Relativity, quantum physics and the new esoteric theory of Chaoism are held to converge and overturn long-held assumptions about life, mind, religion, causality and the universe; 'magic' may be read as psychological and parapsychological technology."@en ;
    skos:note "Insights said to filter down slowly from those who use them practically."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, pp. 7-8; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophBookScheme a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Book scheme: assassinate an idea, seek Apophenia beyond it"@en, "Aufbau: eine Idee töten, jenseits davon Apophänie suchen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Each chapter demolishes a long-held idea (Being, Self, God, Causality, Singularity) and then seeks an Apophenia in an alternative paradigm."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, pp. 8, 10; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophApophenia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Apophenia"@en, "Apophänie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Discovering pattern or meaning that others miss, usually accompanied by feelings of revelation and ecstasy; it links creativity with psychosis and genius with madness, and a talent for it is said to characterise magicians, mystics and occultists. Closely associated with pareidolia."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, p. 8; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophRevelationEcstasySuccess a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Revelation and ecstasy mark Apophenia"@en, "Offenbarung und Ekstase kennzeichnen Apophänie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Genuine pattern-finding is usually accompanied by a feeling of revelation and ecstatic states."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: feelings of revelation and ecstasy usually accompany Apophenia. No deadline; a subjective mark, and the same passage grants that Apophenia also finds pattern where none exists, so the feeling does not separate a true find from a false one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, p. 8; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophApopheniaPsychosisCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Apophenia shades into psychosis and pareidolia"@en, "Apophänie grenzt an Psychose und Pareidolie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The faculty of finding meaning can also find pattern where none exists: mistaking rope for snakes, reading figures into stars and people, unreasonable conspiracy theories and theology of sky fairies."@en ;
    skos:note "Caution against the book's own guiding faculty."@en ;
    skos:note "A warning the work gives against its own guiding faculty."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophApophenia ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, p. 8; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophApopheniaAbsentFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Apophenia fails to answer the call"@en, "Apophänie folgt dem Ruf nicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Inspiration does not always come when invoked; she sometimes rejects seductions and entreaties, sometimes arrives when one is 'out of one's head', and at times Pareidolia, her mad sister, turns up instead."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the muse may not come when called, or her mad sister Pareidolia may come instead. No deadline; nobody is blamed, the failure is put on her caprice; observable only to the one who called, as no inspiration or as false pattern."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, p. 9; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPareidoliaNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Pareidolia, mad sister of Apophenia"@en, "Pareidolie, die wahnsinnige Schwester der Apophänie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Personified as Apophenia's mad sister who sometimes comes instead of her; the faculty of seeing false patterns, yet credited with a part in art and religion."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Pareidolia" ;
    pm:nameRole "mad sister of Apophenia" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, pp. 8-9; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophInnerMythos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Inner Mythos: pantheon of powers within"@en, "Innerer Mythos: Pantheon der inneren Kräfte"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chaoism aims to explore and enlarge the Inner Mythos, i.e. the inner pantheon of powers; the author prefers 'household gods' located inside his own and sometimes other people's heads."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, p. 9; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophOuranosNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ouranos"@en, "Ouranos"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician identity lying past the melodrama of the seven classical drives; pursued by the author for decades as a mythos; Apophenia is its female aspect."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Ouranos" ;
    pm:nameRole "the magician identity beyond the seven classical motivations" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, p. 9; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSevenMotivationsAndOuranos a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Seven classical motivations plus Ouranos"@en, "Sieben klassische Motivationen plus Ouranos"@de ;
    skos:definition "The seven classical planets are paired with motivations (paired as sex and death, fear and desire, love and war, plus solar ego); Uranus-Ouranos stands outside them as the magician identity and counterpoint to the solar ego."@en ;
    skos:note "Planetary attributions called 'fanciful' by the author."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, p. 9; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstMonotheistDeity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against the monotheistic Deity"@en, "Gegen den monotheistischen Gott"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rejects a hyper-inflated-ego capital-D deity who would care personally about trivial observances in a vast universe; calls such belief megalomania by proxy."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with monotheism; the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, p. 9; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophApopheniaGoddessNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Apophenia, goddess"@en, "Apophänie, Göttin"@de ;
    skos:definition "Treated as a goddess and muse, the feminine side of the Ouranian current, regarded conventionally as female because inspiration is linked with right-hemisphere holistic activity; a 'household god' found inside one's own head, who showed the author meaning in a universe run by pure chance. The author claims to sacrifice ideas (Being, Self, God, Causality, Singularity) on her altar."@en ;
    skos:note "Located inside the head (and sometimes inside others' heads) rather than as an external being."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Apophenia" ;
    pm:nameRole "goddess, female side of the Ouranian current; a household god inside one's head" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 1 Apophenia - Introduction, pp. 8-10; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Chapter 2, Panpsychism - Philosophy
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNonBeing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Metaphysics of non-being: phenomena are only doings"@en, "Metaphysik des Nicht-Seins: Phänomene sind nur Tun"@de ;
    skos:definition "No phenomenon has 'being'; all consist of ongoing processes, a stone being nothing but its total activity; space and time are structured by matter rather than a passive stage; 'being' is said to be merely an illusion of nerves and language. The author applies this to himself: he is nothing beyond his total activity."@en ;
    skos:note "Early Buddhist emptiness doctrine cited as anticipation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, pp. 16-18; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstBeingMetaphysics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against western being-metaphysics and popularised Buddhism"@en, "Gegen westliche Seinsmetaphysik und volkstümlichen Buddhismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Western thinkers assumed being and only after centuries found all phenomena change; westerners misread Buddhist illusoriness as devaluing matter for spirit; popular Buddhism is said to bury its austere core ideas in custom and superstition."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with western philosophy of being; popular Buddhism; the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, p. 16; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophBeingPrejudiceCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The concept of being breeds prejudice and false dualism"@en, "Der Seinsbegriff erzeugt Vorurteil und falschen Dualismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Every use of 'to be' is said to hide a false or questionable premise; generalising what groups 'are' leads to irrational thought and ghastly consequences, said to have killed millions; the being-doing split breeds spirit-matter and mind-body dualism, idiotic religious ideas, and self-incomprehension."@en ;
    skos:note "The killing of millions is announced on p. 14 and explained by the group generalisations on p. 17."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstBeingMetaphysics ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, pp. 14, 17-19; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophSpeakingWithoutToBe a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Speaking without 'to be'"@en, "Sprechen ohne 'sein'"@de ;
    skos:definition "Avoid saying any phenomenon 'is' another; describe only actions, resemblances and differences, and treat any statement of what something is as useful only insofar as it implies what it does. The book's own exposition avoids 'am', 'is', 'are' (except in quotation for illustration) and also 'was' (reasons given in ch. 5)."@en ;
    skos:note "Resembles E-Prime, not named as such in this unit."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, pp. 18-19; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophClearThinking a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Clear, prejudice-free thinking about phenomena"@en, "Klares, vorurteilsfreies Denken über Phänomene"@de ;
    skos:definition "Thinking with clarity, avoiding prejudice and false dualisms, and being able to understand quantum behaviour and oneself; claimed as attainable by abandoning the language of being."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, pp. 18-19; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophWithoutToBeYieldsClearThinking a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Speaking without 'to be' yields clear thinking"@en, "Sprechen ohne 'sein' bewirkt klares Denken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Giving up both the word and the idea of being is said to permit clear philosophising and to lead to strict monism."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophSpeakingWithoutToBe ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophClearThinking ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, pp. 18-19; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophLanguageStructuresThought a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Language structures thought"@en, "Sprache strukturiert Denken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Language shapes thought at least as much as it reflects it: an idea without a word is hard to think, words can lend false reality to concepts, and subject-verb-object grammar encourages imagining a subject's being apart from its doing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, p. 19; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophStrictMonism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Strict monism"@en, "Strenger Monismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Abandoning 'being' yields a strict monism with neither a spirit/matter nor a mind/body dualism; a single class of phenomena suffices to account for events, whatever name it gets."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, pp. 19-20; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstEmergentism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against Emergentism"@en, "Gegen den Emergentismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rejects the view, supported by Darwinian theory, that mind is a mere epiphenomenon emerging at a threshold of neural complexity; offers instead that mind is a fundamental property of all matter."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with Emergentism; Darwinian evolutionary theory as its support; the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, pp. 20-21; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPantheismToPanpsychism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "From Pantheism to Panpsychism"@en, "Vom Pantheismus zum Panpsychismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Pantheism held the universe to be the mind of a god of which we are thoughts; once theism drains out and a strict monism is sought, the result is Panpsychism: spirit, mind and matter are one and the same class of phenomena."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, p. 21; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSpiritMatterAsMoral a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spirit-matter duality as disguised moral distinction"@en, "Geist-Materie-Dualität als verkappte Moralunterscheidung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Religions assume good spirit and bad matter, then muddle it with bad spirits and acceptable matter; a universe entirely of spirit or entirely of matter would behave identically, so the distinction is merely moral."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with religions (spirit-matter dualism); the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 1 The Metaphysics of Non-Being, p. 21; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPanpsychism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Panpsychism"@en, "Panpsychismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "If matter naturally includes mind, mind occurs everywhere and creates no paradox; apparent mindlessness of furniture is explained by slowness or by being incoherent aggregates of parts. Lineage claimed from animism and shamanism through Thales, Cardano, Bruno, Spinoza, Leibniz, Schopenhauer, Whitehead and Chalmers."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 2 Panpsychism, p. 22; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophQuantumPanpsychism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Quantum Panpsychism"@en, "Quantenpanpsychismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fundamental quanta of matter and energy display mind-like behaviour; both mind and quanta mix apparently causal and random behaviour; quanta are credited with a rudimentary free will since they behave randomly within bounds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 2 Panpsychism, pp. 23-24; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophStratifiedDeciding a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Deciding by stratified mixture of logic and chance"@en, "Entscheiden durch geschichtete Mischung aus Logik und Zufall"@de ;
    skos:definition "When options have equal appeal, choose randomly or by whim; when no options present themselves, let ideas arise and combine randomly until one fits logically or emotionally; combine these in a layered way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 2 Panpsychism, p. 24; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophFreeWillWithinLimits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Free will as randomness within limits"@en, "Freier Wille als Zufall innerhalb von Grenzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Free will is claimed to consist of reaching decisions within limits that no agency, oneself included, could predict beforehand; machines could be built with any degree of it on the same principle."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 2 Panpsychism, p. 24; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophStratifiedDecidingYieldsFreeWill a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Mixed deterministic-random deciding yields free will"@en, "Gemischtes Entscheiden erzeugt freien Willen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mixing deterministic and random processes in deciding is said to constitute free will."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophStratifiedDeciding ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophFreeWillWithinLimits ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 2 Panpsychism, p. 24; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophLargeNumbersFreeWill a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Law of large numbers and macroscopic free will"@en, "Gesetz der großen Zahl und makroskopischer freier Wille"@de ;
    skos:definition "Random quantum behaviour averages into apparently causal bulk behaviour; but where a few quanta can affect the whole (weather, brain), the whole acts with free will; even a billiard ball becomes indeterminate after about seven cushion bounces."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 2 Panpsychism, pp. 24-25; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPanpsychismTestability a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Testability of Panpsychism and its use for magic"@en, "Prüfbarkeit des Panpsychismus und Nutzen für Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Against the charge that Panpsychism is unfalsifiable and merely mystical, quanta are said to 'remember' and 'communicate' without contact; this may explain brain activity and parapsychological events, and 'perhaps' explain magic and suggest how to improve it."@en ;
    skos:note "Hedged: 'may', 'perhaps'. Statement on the testability of the model."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 2 Panpsychism, pp. 25-26; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstMiraclesAsProof a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against miracles as proof of spirit"@en, "Gegen Wunder als Geistbeweis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Religions treat trivial anomalies as hard evidence; since parapsychological events happen outside religion and within mutually exclusive religions, each religion must ascribe the other's miracles to its own devils."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with spirit-matter dualists; religions; the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 3 Quantum Panpsychism and Magic, pp. 26-27; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophQuantumHomeOfMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Quantum level as the home of magic and free will"@en, "Quantenebene als Heimat von Magie und freiem Willen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Parapsychological events are read as evidence against a spirit separate from matter; magical-seeming events (teleporting, instant communication, superposition, backward travel in time) are frequent at quantum scale; suitably configured aggregates let free will, mind and magic appear macroscopically, while cancelling aggregates appear inert."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 3 Quantum Panpsychism and Magic, p. 27; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMagicOnFluidSystems a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical influence on fluid systems: weather, people, dice"@en, "Magischer Einfluss auf fluide Systeme: Wetter, Menschen, Würfel"@de ;
    skos:definition "Deliberate magic is said to work far better on targets retaining the behavioural fluidity of their quanta: influencing weather, other people's conduct, or how properly thrown dice land."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 3 Quantum Panpsychism and Magic, p. 27; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophInertMatterFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic against inert bulk matter does poorly"@en, "Magie gegen träge Masse wirkt schlecht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Trying to split stones by unaided mental effort gives poor results compared with fluid targets; inert, cancelling aggregates resist magic."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: operations aimed at inert bulk matter, such as splitting stone by mind alone, do poorly. No deadline; the failure is put on the target, whose quanta cancel out, not on the operator; observable as a comparison of results by kind of target."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 3 Quantum Panpsychism and Magic, p. 27; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophGlassFracture a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fracturing glass by poltergeist-type anger"@en, "Glasbruch durch poltergeistartigen Zorn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Middling pieces of glass occasionally give way to mental force, being stressed by cooling and so prone to spontaneous fracture and poltergeist-type activity from people gifted in sharp anger gnosis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 3 Quantum Panpsychism and Magic, pp. 27-28; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophGlassNeedsAngerGnosis a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Glass-breaking presupposes gifted anger gnosis"@en, "Glasbruch setzt Begabung für akute Zorngnosis voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The poltergeist-type effect on glass is ascribed to people gifted in intense anger gnosis."@en ;
    skos:note "The text ascribes the effect to those gifted in acute anger gnosis and sets no sequence of training; the strength is the integrator's weakest reading."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicApophAngerAsGnosis ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicApophGlassFracture ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 3 Quantum Panpsychism and Magic, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMindLikeWithoutConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mind-like behaviour without consciousness"@en, "Geistartiges Verhalten ohne Bewusstsein"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mind-like behaviour is attributed to quanta and thus to everything, but not consciousness; the author sees no basis for ascribing consciousness even to himself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 2 Panpsychism - Philosophy, part 3 Quantum Panpsychism and Magic, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Chapter 3, Multimind - Psychology
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophConsciousnessHasObject a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Consciousness always has an object"@en, "Bewusstsein hat stets einen Gegenstand"@de ;
    skos:definition "Consciousness always focuses on some percept, state, emotion or thought; content-free consciousness does not exist; it is an activity that stops under anaesthesia or in deep sleep."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophInnerBodyAwareness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Awareness of one's own blood circulation, endocrine functions or mystical feelings"@en, "Wahrnehmung des eigenen Blutkreislaufs, endokriner Vorgänge oder mystischer Gefühle"@de ;
    skos:definition "Meditation or sensory deprivation may yield an interesting awareness of one's blood circulation, endocrine functions, or mystical feelings and ideas."@en ;
    skos:note "Practice named but not described."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPureConsciousnessFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Pure consciousness cannot be reached"@en, "Reines Bewusstsein ist unerreichbar"@de ;
    skos:definition "However hard one tries with meditation or sensory deprivation, pure (content-free) consciousness is never achieved."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: content-free consciousness is never reached by meditation or sensory deprivation. No deadline; the failure is put on the aim, impossible in principle, not on the meditator; observable only by introspection."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstCogito a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against Descartes' cogito"@en, "Gegen Descartes' Cogito"@de ;
    skos:definition "'I think therefore I am' uses 'I' twice, which cannot denote the same thing: one part thinks and another observes; introspection implies a dialogue."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with René Descartes; the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', pp. 29-31; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMindAsVerb a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mind as a verb; self-awareness as one part watching another"@en, "Geist als Verb; Selbstgewahrsein als ein Teil, der einen anderen beobachtet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mind is an activity of the brain, not a thing; self-awareness can only be one part of the system aware of another; we learn to assume the same part is always aware, probably as a survival strategy for coherence despite contrary evidence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', pp. 30-31; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTrickOfConsciousnessCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Understanding the trick of consciousness: hell or liberation"@en, "Den Trick des Bewusstseins verstehen: Hölle oder Befreiung"@de ;
    skos:definition "A cited neurophysiologist suggested consciousness is an involuntary self-deception, and grasping it could damn everyone; Buddhist philosophers might instead say such understanding could free us."@en ;
    skos:note "The quip is reported from an unnamed neurophysiologist in a collection of brief essays on unprovable beliefs; the Buddhist answer is the work's own aside."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophMindAsVerb ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', pp. 30-31; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstZombieCamps a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against both camps on the philosophical zombie"@en, "Gegen beide Lager zum philosophischen Zombie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Against those who think a zombie shows consciousness to be illusory and those who think humans need a singular extra ingredient, the author suspects a creature with only a single consciousness would act like the zombie; consciousness is incomprehensible if assumed singular."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with theorists of the philosophical zombie (both camps); the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', pp. 30-32; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPluralConsciousnesses a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Plural consciousnesses"@en, "Plurale Bewusstseine"@de ;
    skos:definition "Each quale or state (pain, anger, normal waking) behaves like a separate consciousness; as one grows active, others recede and it becomes the vantage from which one observes oneself. Assuming many consciousnesses makes the flitting and simultaneity of states comprehensible."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', pp. 33-34; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophExtremeStatesAsOtherConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Extreme states and inspiration as other consciousnesses"@en, "Extremzustände und Inspiration als andere Bewusstseine"@de ;
    skos:definition "Extreme meditation, concentration or mystical practice reportedly reduces awareness of everything else; people often disown such states afterwards and ascribe them to spirits, and creative people locate inspiration in a source outside normal consciousness, which, when very active, can observe the receding normal consciousness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', p. 33; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAngerTakeoverCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Anger consciousness can take over"@en, "Das Zornbewusstsein kann übernehmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In extreme anger, the anger consciousness may converse directly with body consciousness while normal consciousness shuts down; those unused to such states may afterwards be unable to explain or remember their actions and may disclaim agency."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophAngerAsGnosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', p. 33; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophBrainHostsConsciousnesses a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Brain as a host of many consciousnesses"@en, "Gehirn als Wirt vieler Bewusstseine"@de ;
    skos:definition "Under quantum panpsychism a philosophical zombie is impossible: any device complex enough to monitor itself and its environment has consciousnesses; the brain supports many, some monitoring others, none monitoring all; a coalition of the more active ones learns to call itself singular consciousness in (post-)monotheist cultures, at great effort."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', p. 34; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophLooseningUnitaryConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gains from loosening the unitary consciousness"@en, "Gewinn durch Lockerung des einheitlichen Bewusstseins"@de ;
    skos:definition "Much is said to be gained in magic, mysticism and creative thinking by loosening the hold of the learned unitary consciousness; part 3 addresses undoing the self."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 1 The Myth of 'Consciousness', pp. 34-35; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSelfAsSocialConstruct a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Self as social construct"@en, "Selbst als soziales Konstrukt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The self is assembled from genes, language, ideas and behaviours absorbed from others, with some later choice among cultural add-ons; theory of mind, first applied to others, is turned inward; pressure to behave consistently and human suggestibility make us internalise a singular self."@en ;
    skos:note "Also: a significant other who behaves wildly inconsistently may lead to eccentric or dysfunctional ideas of self and others. References Norretranders and Ornstein."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, pp. 35-36; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophBeliefFollowsAction a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Belief follows action"@en, "Glaube folgt dem Handeln"@de ;
    skos:definition "Persistently acting out the behaviour belonging to a belief instils it more strongly than anything; we come to believe what we do rather than do what we believe."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, p. 36; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTheologyImpliesPsychology a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Every theology implies a psychology"@en, "Jede Theologie impliziert eine Psychologie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Pantheons and demonologies map selves: pagan cultures provided a deity for nearly every activity, allowing impulses expression with less inner conflict and less personal agency; Greek and Roman culture gave muse, daemon and genius alike as semi-autonomous wellsprings of inspiration."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, pp. 36-37; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophDaemonsIntoDemons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Monotheism turns daemons into demons"@en, "Monotheismus macht Daimonen zu Dämonen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Monotheism splits behaviour into approved and damned, serves social control, and recasts the inspiring daemons of the pagans as demons to be rejected as not-self, producing a flourishing trade in guilt and self-hatred; post-monotheist secular democracies keep the mono-self and its demands for consistency."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with monotheism; post-monotheist secularism; the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, pp. 37-38; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophVoicesPossessionPersonae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Voices, possession and multiple personae"@en, "Stimmen, Besessenheit und multiple Personae"@de ;
    skos:definition "Many hear voices without dysfunction, using them as inspiration, founding religious ideas on them, or becoming mediums or occultists; possession is a sought state in Voodoo and other ecstatic cults; anyone can present different personae, and clinical multiple personality with amnesia is rare."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, p. 39; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTraumaSplitCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Trauma can fix a split between personae"@en, "Trauma kann eine Spaltung der Personae verfestigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Grave trauma may fix a lasting division between personae that are otherwise normally shiftable."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophVoicesPossessionPersonae ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, p. 39; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstRigidPsyche a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against rigid divisions of the psyche"@en, "Gegen starre Einteilungen der Psyche"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sharp divisions of conscious, subconscious, unconscious and super-conscious, and the 'ego', arose in a more repressed culture and now look artificial; impulses and memories differ only in their tendency to seize control of the organism."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with classical depth psychology (Freud and early theorists); the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, p. 39; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMindConfirmsAnyScheme a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The mind confirms any scheme imposed on it"@en, "Der Geist bestätigt jedes ihm auferlegte Schema"@de ;
    skos:definition "Whatever descriptive map is laid on the mind tends to be confirmed by it, be it Freud's Id/Ego/Superego, the Kabbalistic Sephiroth (Tree of Life), or Wilson and Leary's eight-circuit model; no part can comprehend the whole malleable assemblage."@en ;
    skos:note "Statement on the testedness of psychological and magical maps."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, pp. 39-40; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMonoMultiSelfSpectrum a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spectrum from Mono-Self to Multi-Self"@en, "Spektrum vom Mono-Selbst zum Multi-Selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "Humans range from the Mono-Self type (predictable, restricted creativity, a cellar of demons and discarded angels) to the Multi-Self type (creative and unpredictable, but erratic if communication breaks down); neither extreme is optimal since both erect barriers between selves."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, p. 40; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAutomatonRandomatonCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Steer between automaton and disintegrated Randomaton"@en, "Zwischen Automat und zerfallenem Randomaton steuern"@de ;
    skos:definition "To explore inner riches and come out functional and sane, one must aim between the zombie-like mono-self and the disintegrated complete Randomaton; the full Multi-Self becomes dysfunctional if communication between selves fails."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophMonoMultiSelfSpectrum, pmp:ChaosMagicApophStochasticSelfManagement ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, p. 40; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMonotheistMysticismFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Monotheist mysticism ends in the demon realms"@en, "Monotheistische Mystik endet in den Dämonenreichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Exalting one imagined god-self by repressing all natural ungodliness never succeeds, perhaps until old age erodes appetites; meanwhile urges may be sublimated into good deeds, yet one should anticipate eruptions of terrible conduct, or at least sleepless guilt and anguish to no purpose."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the monotheist mystic who represses all ungodliness never succeeds, perhaps until old age wears the appetites down. The only time mark is old age; the failure is put on the monotheist method; observable as outbreaks of bad conduct or long nights of guilt."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, p. 40; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophLegionNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Legion"@en, "Legion"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Devil's self-given name is read as the legion of repressed selves kept in the monotheist's dungeons."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Legion" ;
    pm:nameRole "the name the Devil gave, read as the repressed selves of the monotheist" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 2 Constructing the Self, p. 40; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophStochasticSelfManagement a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Stochastic multi-self management"@en, "Stochastisches Management der Selbste"@de ;
    skos:definition "If one self does not work in a situation, try another; if necessary choose which at random."@en ;
    skos:note "Called lateral thinking on a grand scale."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 41; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophNewNameNewSelf a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Taking a new name for a new self"@en, "Neuer Name für ein neues Selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "On entering a new situation, accept or adopt a different name and let the self that goes with it act there, keeping it separate from one's usual identity outside that setting."@en ;
    skos:note "Mystical orders' inner names cited as parallel."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 41; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSituationalWorkingSelf a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A distinct, situation-bound working self"@en, "Ein eigenständiges, situationsgebundenes Arbeitsselbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "A separately named self that handles a situation better than the usual identity and is not carried home afterwards."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 41; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNewNameYieldsWorkingSelf a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "New name yields a better working self (Jim)"@en, "Neuer Name bringt ein besseres Arbeitsselbst (Jim)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Being called Jim for eighteen months at a job, the author found Jim educated unwilling and difficult pupils better than Pete could have; Pete in turn declined to take Jim's identity home."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophNewNameNewSelf ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophSituationalWorkingSelf ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 41; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNewSituation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Entering a new situation where one is not yet known"@en, "Eintritt in eine neue Lage, in der man noch nicht bekannt ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "A setting such as a new job, entered among people who have no prior name for one, in which a different name can take hold from the first day."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 41; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophRenamingNeedsNewSituation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Renaming works best on entering a new situation"@en, "Umbenennung wirkt am besten beim Eintritt in eine neue Situation"@de ;
    skos:definition "The renaming technique works best when entering a new setting."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence of skipping is the passage counted as a condition of failure under ChaosMagicApophRenamingAmongAcquaintancesFailure."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicApophNewSituation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophNewNameNewSelf ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Asking people who already know you to use a different name has little short-term effect and gains no extra freedom." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 41; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophRenamingAmongAcquaintancesFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Renaming among acquaintances fails"@en, "Umbenennung unter Bekannten scheitert"@de ;
    skos:definition "Requesting a new name from people who already know you does little in the short run and gains no extra degree of freedom."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: asking existing acquaintances to use a new name gains little in the short term and no extra freedom. The short term is not quantified; the failure is put on the setting chosen; observable, though the gain it misses is not measured."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 41; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophNamingInnerSelves a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Naming one's inner selves (personal mythos)"@en, "Benennen der Selbste des persönlichen Mythos"@de ;
    skos:definition "Let the selves name one another or swap names, and welcome one another as co-owners of a party with no privileged host; lacking psychological terms, use names of safely dead classical gods; run one's life as a party offering something to each self."@en ;
    skos:note "Most people have no names for their selves; the author deems current psychology primitive."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 42; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNamingSelvesInsanityCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Naming selves may look like the road to insanity"@en, "Selbste zu benennen kann wie der Weg in den Wahnsinn wirken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Naming inner selves might seem the first step toward madness and the breakup of ego or self-image; the text raises the question of who does the naming and answers that the selves must name each other."@en ;
    skos:note "The work answers its own question: with no essential inner core, the selves must name each other."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicApophNamingInnerSelves ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 42; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophInnerDemocracy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Inner democracy"@en, "Innere Demokratie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Modelled on democracy (least-bad governance, which depends on blocs tolerating each other and negotiating), a truly sane individual seeks a compromise among all its impulses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 42; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophUnadvisedMarsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "An unadvised Mars self leads to disaster"@en, "Ein unberatenes Mars-Selbst führt ins Verderben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Everyone has a self that relishes violence; left unadvised by the other selves it rapidly leads the whole organism to disaster. Watching violent entertainment titillates without satisfying, maintaining the pretence that one abhors violence."@en ;
    skos:note "Directed at the violent self let out without the counsel of the others; the ritual exercise of Wotan is the author's managed form of the same self."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicApophWotanRitualExercise ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, pp. 42-43; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophWotanRitualExercise a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Regular ritual exercise of the Wotan self"@en, "Regelmäßige rituelle Übung des Wotan-Selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "Release the violent self from its cage for regular ritual exercise: weightlifting, sword practice, drums and cannon, axe on shield, fire and explosions, muscle-powered projectiles, and working up an ecstatic rage for its own sake."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 43; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophWotanNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Wotan"@en, "Wotan"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's name for his self that loves violence and rage; a valuable committee member who plays complex wargames with Logicus."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Wotan" ;
    pm:nameRole "the self that loves violence; a committee member of the author's selves" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 43; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAngerAsGnosis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Anger as resource and gnosis"@en, "Zorn als Ressource und Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Anger can temporarily double physical strength and concentration in hard work, radiate a crazed charisma that wins confrontations by intimidation, and act as a gnosis for sending intent magically."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 43; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophIntegratedViolentSelf a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A trusted, integrated violent self"@en, "Ein vertrauenswürdiges, integriertes Gewalt-Selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The violent self becomes a trusted committee member that does not act out of turn and is aggressive only at organised stupidity and malice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 43; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophWotanExerciseYieldsIntegratedAnger a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ritual exercise of Wotan yields an integrated anger self and usable anger"@en, "Rituelle Übung Wotans ergibt integriertes Zornselbst und nutzbaren Zorn"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author reports that regularly exercising Wotan leaves a self he can trust and whose anger can serve as a resource and gnosis."@en ;
    skos:note "Link to anger-as-resource rests on adjacency in the text."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophWotanRitualExercise ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophIntegratedViolentSelf, pmc:ChaosMagicApophAngerAsGnosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 43; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophLogicusNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Logicus"@en, "Logicus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's abstract-thinking self, who would neither rationalise away the hatreds nor kill the loves."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Logicus" ;
    pm:nameRole "the abstract thinker among the author's selves" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 43; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSelvesAsGods a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Each self has magical powers / can act as a god"@en, "Jedes Selbst hat magische Kräfte / kann als Gott wirken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Every self appears to possess magical power when the rest step back and let it work; any of them may act as a god for a while once the others cease holding it back."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSelvesStandAside a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Other selves must stand aside"@en, "Die anderen Selbste müssen zurücktreten"@de ;
    skos:definition "A self's magical power or god-form manifests only if the other selves step aside and stop restraining it."@en ;
    skos:note "The prior step is the practice of letting one self manifest in full god form while the others step back, which the work gives at the chairless round table."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophChairlessRoundTable ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicApophSelvesAsGods ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophInnerCommittee a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "The author's inner committee of selves"@en, "Das innere Komitee des Autors"@de ;
    skos:definition "Wotan (violence), Logicus (abstract thought), Death (saturnine advisor on time and mortality), Sex (polymorphous, with harmless paraphilias), several Loves and several Hatreds each with different objects and payoffs; recognising Loves and Hatreds as plural reportedly ended much confusion."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, pp. 43-44; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophDeathSelfNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Death (inner self)"@en, "Tod (inneres Selbst)"@de ;
    skos:definition "An inner self giving saturnine advice on time, ageing, mortality and futility."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Death" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the author's selves, giving saturnine advice" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophChairlessRoundTable a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Chairless round table of selves"@en, "Runder Tisch der Selbste ohne Vorsitz"@de ;
    skos:definition "Give no self the chair; pass the 'microphone' among selves as circumstances dictate, or at random when none do; occasionally let one self manifest fully in god form (developed in ch. 4)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophFundamentalSelfAsChaos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fundamental self as quantum chaos"@en, "Grundlegendes Selbst als Quantenchaos"@de ;
    skos:definition "Asking which self one 'is' is meaningless (it smuggles in being); if any fundamental self exists it is the quantum panpsychic chaos beneath all one's consciousnesses, echoing the Greek idea that gods arose from Chaos."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTeamOfSelves a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Functioning better as a team of selves"@en, "Besseres Funktionieren als Team der Selbste"@de ;
    skos:definition "Making the most of all contained selves; the author reports functioning better by treating himself as a team and occasionally letting one self manifest in full god form."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophRoundTableYieldsTeamOfSelves a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Round table and stochastic self management yield teamwork of selves"@en, "Runder Tisch und stochastisches Selbstmanagement ergeben Teamarbeit der Selbste"@de ;
    skos:definition "Treating selves as a chairless team, passing control by circumstance or chance, is reported to make the author function better."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophChairlessRoundTable, pmp:ChaosMagicApophStochasticSelfManagement, pmp:ChaosMagicApophNamingInnerSelves ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophTeamOfSelves, pmc:ChaosMagicApophLooseningUnitaryConsciousness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophConsistencyBreedsMediocrity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Self-consistency breeds mediocrity"@en, "Selbstkonsistenz erzeugt Mittelmaß"@de ;
    skos:definition "Great people hold great contradictions; being consistent with oneself is no virtue and only produces mediocrity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, part 3 Dicing with the Randomaton, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophHumansAndGodsMakeEachOther a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Humans and gods shape one another"@en, "Menschen und Götter erschaffen einander"@de ;
    skos:definition "Caption to the illustration showing chimeras of a neural new pantheon: we hold worlds and others within, and humans and gods fashion one another after their own likeness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 3 Multimind - Psychology, illustration caption, p. 45; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Chapter 4, Neopantheism - DIY Religion
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMysticalCapability a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mystical capability"@en, "Mystische Fähigkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ability to find awe and wonder in nature, in consciousness, or in the bare fact that anything exists; contrasted with a mere 'religious capability' that wants answers to believe in and accepts absurdity over uncertainty."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 46; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophReligiousInstinctCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The religious instinct is easily subverted"@en, "Der religiöse Instinkt ist leicht zu unterwandern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Because of human suggestibility the religious instinct is readily hijacked for social and political control or for priestly livelihoods; most religion is said to resemble mental illness, narrowing horizons, taking metaphor literally, preferring faith to evidence and imposing certainty."@en ;
    skos:note "The work contrasts a mystical capability with a merely religious one, and the warning falls on the latter."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophMysticalCapability ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 46; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAnusiteParody a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Parody of organised religion (the Anusites)"@en, "Parodie organisierter Religion (die Anusiten)"@de ;
    skos:definition "A deliberately absurd invented religion is offered to show that organised religions are no more coherent, and that mass belief compels respect through threat of violence once it gains secular power."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with organised religion; the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophLogosAndMythos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Logos and Mythos modes of belief"@en, "Logos- und Mythos-Modus des Glaubens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Gods may be believed in Logos style, as objectively existing independent entities, or Mythos style, as metaphorical principles explaining world and heart; Egyptians are thought to have blended the two, while Plato and most Greek thinkers distinguished them and read myths as metaphor."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', pp. 47-48; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstLogocentricMonotheism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against logocentric monotheism"@en, "Gegen logozentrischen Monotheismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "From the Council of Nicea, a religion fixed on the literal truth of a canon suppressed both mythological thinking and reasoned enquiry, ushering in the Dark Ages; Islam is said to use the same sacred-book technique; fundamentalism persists."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with Christianity after Nicea; Islam; fundamentalism; the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', pp. 49-50; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophParadigmQuadrants a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Logos/Mythos x Observation/Belief quadrant scheme (Figure 1)"@en, "Quadrantenschema Logos/Mythos x Beobachtung/Glaube (Abb. 1)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two axes: Logos-Mythos and Observation-Belief. Logos+Observation = Science; Logos+Belief = Fundamentalism; Mythos+Observation = Magic (any mythos-style thinking about observed phenomena, incl. astrology and alchemy); Mythos+Belief = Pantheism (analogical attitude to belief). Schools of thought occupy regions of the graph."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', pp. 50-51; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPsychogram a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Paradigm footprint (psychogram) and the Chaoist position"@en, "Paradigmen-Fußabdruck (Psychogramm) und die chaoistische Position"@de ;
    skos:definition "Each person or school has a footprint on the quadrant graph; the average Briton sits near the origin. Chaoist philosophy centres on the Magic quadrant, regarding existence as random but open to being foreseen and steered psychically and physically, and to being steered by belief, with tendrils into Science and Pantheism, avoiding the 'Sky Fairy' quadrant of literal gods and spirits."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 52; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophQuadrantsTestedByResults a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Each quadrant tested by results except fundamentalism"@en, "Jeder Quadrant wird an Ergebnissen geprüft, außer Fundamentalismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Science makes material things happen or is wrong; magic yields useful results or not; pantheism supplies an agreeable narrative or not; only fundamentalism is maintained by faith alone against evidence."@en ;
    skos:note "Statement on how magic is to be tested: by useful results."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 52; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNoUsefulResultsFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic without useful results / pantheism without a livable narrative has failed"@en, "Magie ohne nützliche Ergebnisse / Pantheismus ohne tragfähige Erzählung ist gescheitert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The failure criterion for magic is the absence of useful results; for pantheism, failing to supply a pleasing story for living."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the work's own criterion that magic which gives no useful results, and pantheism which gives no narrative to live by, have failed. No deadline or count; the failure is put on the paradigm in use; observable, the one test the work states for magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 52; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophFaithAsContraEvidential a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Faith as contra-evidential investment"@en, "Glaube als widerevidenzielle Investition"@de ;
    skos:definition "Only irrational beliefs can work for literal religion, since people invest emotion only in questionable claims; faith lives in constant dialogue with doubt, must mostly fail to deliver to keep attracting investment, and fears blasphemy and apostasy; prayer is described as persuading oneself of something known to be false and asking it for favours."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with fundamentalism; literal religion; the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', pp. 52-53; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophGodsFromTheoryOfMind a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gods arise from theory of mind"@en, "Götter entstehen aus der Theory of Mind"@de ;
    skos:definition "Belief in literally real gods and spirits comes from the faculty that models other minds; others, real or 'imaginary' (anyone not met), exist for us to the extent we invite them into or they force their way into our heads; identities are built by dialogue with and accretion from such figures."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', pp. 53-54; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMindParasitesCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Mind parasites"@en, "Geistesparasiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Heavy cultural suggestion in youth can derail the theory-of-mind faculty, installing large parasites in the mind (rulers, gods, priests, dictators, gurus) who strive to control media and tolerate no competition; uncensored media weaken them."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophGodsFromTheoryOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 54; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophInnerPopulation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Filling the head with many real and imaginary people"@en, "Den Kopf mit vielen realen und imaginären Personen füllen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Deliberately take in a broad range of actual and imaginary persons with very different identities through media and acquaintance, holding dialogue with them inwardly."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 54; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophLargerSelfImage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A much larger self-image"@en, "Ein viel größeres Selbstbild"@de ;
    skos:definition "A much enlarged self-image, as opposed to retreat into dialogue with a single god or cult figure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 54; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophInnerPopulationYieldsLargerSelfImage a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Diverse inner population yields a larger self-image"@en, "Vielfältige innere Bevölkerung ergibt größeres Selbstbild"@de ;
    skos:definition "Filling the head with radically different real and imaginary people is said to yield a much larger self-image."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophInnerPopulation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophLargerSelfImage ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 54; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNoMetaphorForSelfCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Lost without a metaphor for self"@en, "Verloren ohne Metapher für das Selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "Faced with freedom of belief, some cling to single creeds, some drift from fad to fad without a metaphor for self, and some exist only through relationships and all but vanish from their own minds when left unengaged."@en ;
    skos:note "Directed at the drift the work describes, a self without metaphor, of which the filling of the head with many figures is the remedy offered on the page before."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicApophInnerPopulation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 55; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNeoPantheismFifthAeon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Neo-Pantheism as spirituality of the Fifth Aeon"@en, "Neo-Pantheismus als Spiritualität des Fünften Äons"@de ;
    skos:definition "New-Age, Neo-Paganism and Chaos Magic share a current of Neo-Pantheism that is taking the place of monotheism and of nihilistic scientific positivism in the dawning Fifth Aeon; hostility from both fundamentalism and science is taken as a sign of the threat it poses them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', pp. 55-56; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophFourSensesOfPantheism a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Four historical senses of Pantheism"@en, "Vier historische Bedeutungen von Pantheismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "(1) a divine power shows itself in everything; (2) gods and spirits permeate every part of the cosmos; (3) god cannot be told apart from nature and is no person; (4) the cosmos in its entirety possesses consciousness or life."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 1 Against Logos, 'The Literal Word', p. 56; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophEightThemesOfNeoPantheism a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eight themes of Neo-Pantheism"@en, "Acht Themen des Neo-Pantheismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "1 nothing true / all permitted; 2 belief and intent make reality; 3 alchemy; 4 the female perspective; 5 synchronicity and meaning; 6 sky fairies versus psi fairies; 7 personal narrative and mythos; 8 cosmic holism and transcendence."@en ;
    skos:note "Presented in most extreme form; few except hardcore mystics accept all; New Age holds diluted forms; some Neo-Pagans build their own fundamentalisms."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, pp. 56-62; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNothingIsTrue a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "'Nothing is true' formula"@en, "Nichts ist wahr, alles ist erlaubt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A deliberately self-contradicting formula meaning that everything holds true only relative to some chosen sense of truth: all truths are provisional and context-dependent, not a contempt for reason. Science at its best is provisional, cannot prove impossibility, and is treated as a source of possibility and ideas to borrow."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 1 Nothing is True, Everything is Permitted, pp. 57-58; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNeopantheistStanceOnReligions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Neopantheist stance toward religions"@en, "Neopantheistische Haltung zu Religionen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sympathy for parts of Hinduism, Shamanism, Paganism and some Mahayana Buddhism for their symbolism, techniques and flexible dogma; contempt for the three Abrahamic monotheisms, since whatever calls itself absolutely true is taken as false; interest limited to Kabbalah, Essene mysticism, Gnosticism and apocrypha."@en ;
    skos:note "A dispute of the work with the Abrahamic monotheisms; the other side is not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 1 Nothing is True, Everything is Permitted, p. 57; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophBeliefAndIntentCreateReality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Belief and intent create reality: the one secret of magic"@en, "Glaube und Absicht erschaffen Wirklichkeit: das einzige Geheimnis der Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Belief and intent creating reality is called the sole secret of magic, mysticism and positive thinking; not absolutely true, but said to work astonishingly well much of the time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 2 Belief and Intent create Reality, p. 58; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophRandomUniverseCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Belief cannot make a random universe fully comply"@en, "Glaube kann ein zufälliges Universum nicht völlig fügen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The principle is not absolutely true: in a random universe we cannot always make it do exactly what we want."@en ;
    skos:note "Self-warning about the book's own core method."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophBeliefAndIntentCreateReality, pmp:ChaosMagicApophBeliefAsTool ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 2 Belief and Intent create Reality, p. 58; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNegativeThinkingFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Negative thinking as counter-test"@en, "Negatives Denken als Gegenprobe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Doubters are dared to think negatively for some time and watch the consequences, implying that negative belief produces bad outcomes."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the doubter is invited to think negatively for some time and watch the outcome, implying bad outcomes. No deadline beyond a while; the failure is put on the thinker's belief; observable only loosely, with no comparison set up."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 2 Belief and Intent create Reality, p. 58; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSituationalChangeByBelief a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Changing a situation through belief and intent (personal empowerment)"@en, "Eine Lage durch Glaube und Absicht verändern (persönliche Ermächtigung)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Developing beliefs and intents able to change a situation; personal empowerment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 2 Belief and Intent create Reality, p. 58; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophImaginationFirst a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Imagination must be enticed first"@en, "Zuerst muss die Imagination gelockt werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Courage, imagination and discipline are needed to develop situation-changing belief and intent; imagination above all has first to be coaxed and encouraged."@en ;
    skos:note "The prior step is read as the practice of choosing beliefs that entice and stimulate imagination, which the same paragraph gives as the consequence."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophBeliefAsTool ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicApophSituationalChangeByBelief ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 2 Belief and Intent create Reality, p. 58; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophBeliefAsTool a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Belief as a tool: acting 'as if' true"@en, "Glaube als Werkzeug: 'als ob' wahr handeln"@de ;
    skos:definition "Treat belief as a tool, not an end; choose beliefs that appeal to and further stimulate imagination, and act them out ritually as if true, so as to build situation-changing belief and intent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 2 Belief and Intent create Reality, p. 58; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophBeliefAsToolYieldsChange a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Acting beliefs 'as if' true yields situational change"@en, "Glauben 'als ob' wahr ausleben bewirkt Veränderung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Selecting imagination-stimulating beliefs and ritually acting them out as if true serves to build the beliefs and intents that change situations."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophBeliefAsTool ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophSituationalChangeByBelief ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 2 Belief and Intent create Reality, p. 58; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAlchemicalGoldFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Why alchemical aims failed"@en, "Warum alchemistische Ziele scheiterten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Would-be gold-makers mostly failed since they were unable to focus sufficient energy on their substances (though learning metallurgy and chemistry); inner transmuters obtained mixed results."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: gold-making alchemists failed for want of enough energy on their materials; the inner transmuters got mixed results. A historical verdict without deadline; the failure is put on a physical limit, not on the method's theory; observable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 3 Alchemy, pp. 58-59; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophChymicalMarriage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chymical Marriage and Soror Mystica"@en, "Chymische Hochzeit und Soror Mystica"@de ;
    skos:definition "Spiritual alchemists learned the significance of the Chymical Marriage: including the feminine perspective and working with a mystical sister or wife."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 3 Alchemy, pp. 58-59; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPoisonousElixirsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Elixirs that poison"@en, "Elixiere, die vergiften"@de ;
    skos:definition "Alchemists seeking vitality or immortality sometimes achieved the opposite through heavy metal poisoning."@en ;
    skos:note "Directed at the alchemical pursuit of vitality and immortality, of which the placebo discovery is the other outcome named."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophIntentHealing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 3 Alchemy, p. 59; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophIntentHealing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Intent-based (placebo) healing"@en, "Absichtsbasierte (Placebo-)Heilung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Healing effects from intent and placebo, said to be astonishing; placebos given with full medical ritual are said often to outperform 'actual' treatments, especially medication."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 3 Alchemy, p. 59; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophControlledTestFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Alternative medicine fails under controlled testing"@en, "Alternativmedizin versagt unter kontrollierten Bedingungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Alternative health practices often perform poorly in scientifically controlled situations, since they only work as a whole bundle under their own conditions."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: alternative health practices often do poorly under scientific control because they work only as a whole package. No deadline; the failure is put on the test conditions, which shields the practice from a controlled test; observable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 3 Alchemy, p. 59; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophHealingRitualPackage a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Healing as a complete ritual package"@en, "Heilen als vollständiges Ritualpaket"@de ;
    skos:definition "Deliver an intent-based treatment as a whole package on its own terms, with its own analogical or immaterial theory and all its paraphernalia (snake bones, crystals), or with full medical ritual for placebos, to bolster belief."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 3 Alchemy, p. 59; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophHealingNeedsPackage a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Intent healing needs its whole package"@en, "Absichtsheilung braucht ihr ganzes Paket"@de ;
    skos:definition "Intent-based treatments must operate as an entire package on their own terms."@en ;
    skos:note "The prior step is the package practice itself."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophHealingRitualPackage ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicApophIntentHealing ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Stripped of their package, as in controlled trials, they often fail to perform well." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 3 Alchemy, p. 59; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophRitualPackageYieldsHealing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ritual package yields placebo healing"@en, "Ritualpaket ergibt Placebo-Heilung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Treatment delivered with full ritual and supporting theory is said to produce the placebo or intent-based healing effect."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophHealingRitualPackage ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophIntentHealing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 3 Alchemy, p. 59; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophFemalePerspective a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The female perspective"@en, "Die weibliche Perspektive"@de ;
    skos:definition "Neopantheism values intuition, dreams, psychic experience, empathy and the goddess archetype equally with logic, waking thought, rational analysis, objectivity and the god; its rejection of male logocentric monotheisms mirrors this sympathy."@en ;
    skos:note "The author hedges that a man defining it seems presumptuous."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 4 The Female Perspective, p. 59; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophReadingSynchronicity a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Reading synchronicity and reusing working superstitions"@en, "Synchronizität deuten und bewährten Aberglauben wiederverwenden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rely on personal experience over socially sanctioned reality; reuse any superstition that gives good results; do not dismiss coincidence; when a synchronicity appears spontaneously, read it as confirming one's deep intent or as a subconscious warning."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 5 Synchronicity and Meaning, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophEngineeringCoincidence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic as engineering coincidence of intent and actuality"@en, "Magie als Herbeiführen von Koinzidenz zwischen Absicht und Wirklichkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Most of life is spent trying to bring intent and actual events into coincidence; spontaneous synchronicity is read in that light."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 5 Synchronicity and Meaning, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSynchronicityAffirmsIntentSuccess a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spontaneous synchronicity as confirmation of deep intent"@en, "Spontane Synchronizität als Bestätigung tiefer Absicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "A synchronicity arising unbidden may be taken as a sign that one's deep intent is being affirmed (or alternatively as a subconscious warning)."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: a synchronicity that appears unbidden may be read as affirming one's deep intent, or else as a warning from the subconscious. No deadline; observable as an event, but since either reading is open, no coincidence can count against the intent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 5 Synchronicity and Meaning, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophCommonSenseExhausted a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The possibilities of common sense exhausted"@en, "Die Möglichkeiten des gesunden Menschenverstands ausgeschöpft"@de ;
    skos:definition "The state in which the ordinary sensible options for a problem have all been tried without result, named as the point after which magical thinking pays."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 5 Synchronicity and Meaning, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMagicalThinkingResults a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Results from magical thinking"@en, "Ergebnisse durch magisches Denken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magical thinking is said often to produce excellent results once common sense has been exhausted, despite scientific derision."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 5 Synchronicity and Meaning, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSynchronicityYieldsResults a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Synchronicity reading yields results of magical thinking"@en, "Synchronizitätsdeutung ergibt Ergebnisse magischen Denkens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Treating coincidences as meaningful and reusing successful superstitions is the magical thinking said to give excellent results."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophReadingSynchronicity ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophMagicalThinkingResults ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 5 Synchronicity and Meaning, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMagicalThinkingAfterCommonSense a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical thinking after common sense is exhausted"@en, "Magisches Denken erst nach Ausschöpfen des gesunden Menschenverstands"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magical thinking is said to give excellent results when the possibilities of common sense have been exhausted."@en ;
    skos:note "The work gives no consequence for using magical thinking before common sense is exhausted, so the order is entered as prescribed, not as a necessity."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicApophCommonSenseExhausted ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicApophMagicalThinkingResults ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 5 Synchronicity and Meaning, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSpiritsYesNoYes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spirits: yes, no, and yes again"@en, "Geister: ja, nein und wieder ja"@de ;
    skos:definition "Yes psychologically: people's gods and demons do much of their talking and pass between persons; we make them and they make us. No: consciousness is a property of matter, so no fully discarnate beings. Yes again: via parapsychology and quantum connections consciousnesses affect each other across space and time, so laws of nature act as simple powerful discarnate spirits and thoughts as weaker ones."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 6 Sky Fairies or Psi Fairies?, pp. 60-61; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSkyFairiesNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Sky-fairies"@en, "Himmelsfeen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's name for gods and spirits taken as literally real in the Logos sense; such beings are said to dwell only inside people's heads."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Sky-fairies" ;
    pm:nameRole "gods and spirits believed in the Logos sense" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 6 Sky Fairies or Psi Fairies?, p. 61; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPsiFairiesNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Psi-fairies"@en, "Psi-Feen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's name for what one consciousness projects into another; such projections are said to produce effects like those of spirits in the classical sense."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Psi-fairies" ;
    pm:nameRole "spirits sent from one consciousness into another" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 6 Sky Fairies or Psi Fairies?, p. 61; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophPersonalMythos a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Building an elaborate personal narrative and mythos"@en, "Aufbau einer reichen persönlichen Erzählung und Mythologie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Assemble an extensive personal mythos as vocabulary for the inner life, e.g. astrological configuration, clan animal, dimly recalled former lives, a guiding spirit, several servitors, a mission, possible future incarnations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 7 Personal Narrative and Mythos, p. 61; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPoeticInnerLife a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A large, flexible, poetic inner life"@en, "Ein großes, flexibles, poetisches Innenleben"@de ;
    skos:definition "A large and flexible inner life with a rich vocabulary for exploring it, a life of poetry rather than prose."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 7 Personal Narrative and Mythos, p. 61; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPersonalMythosYieldsInnerLife a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Personal mythos yields a poetic inner life"@en, "Persönlicher Mythos ergibt poetisches Innenleben"@de ;
    skos:definition "An elaborate personal narrative is said to furnish a large, flexible inner life."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophPersonalMythos ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophPoeticInnerLife ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 7 Personal Narrative and Mythos, p. 61; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTechnicallyIneptFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Failure marks one as technically inept"@en, "Scheitern weist als technisch unfähig aus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Within the neopantheist paradigm, being unable to make magical thinking work does not prove madness but qualifies one as technically inept."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: within the neopantheist paradigm one who cannot make magical thinking work counts as technically inept, not mad. No deadline; the failure is put on the operator; observable as an outcome, but with no standard of what working would be."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 7 Personal Narrative and Mythos, p. 61; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophCosmicHolism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Cosmic holism"@en, "Kosmischer Holismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Most Neopantheists like to think the cosmos in its entirety has a consciousness we can interact with, seeks greater complexity and consciousness and could use our help, that all species merit preservation, and that a circularity in time links consciousness and the universe's existence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, part 2 Neo-Pantheism, theme 8 Cosmic Holism and Transcendence, p. 62; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophDiceWorlds a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dice worlds: fractal indeterminacy from quantum to cosmos"@en, "Würfelwelten: fraktale Unbestimmtheit vom Quant zum Kosmos"@de ;
    skos:definition "Caption: dice-worlds and fractal self-resemblance across scales from quanta to cosmos; uncertainty among the perpendicular parts of angular momentum, glossed with a quaternion relation; 'she' does spin dice."@en ;
    skos:note "Anticipates ch. 5."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 4 Neopantheism - DIY Religion, illustration caption, p. 63; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

#
# Chapter 5, Metadynamics - Practical Magic
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophUncausedQuantumEvents a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Uncaused quantum events"@en, "Unverursachte Quantenereignisse"@de ;
    skos:definition "Radioactive decay is offered as a natural instance of genuinely random, causeless events; at the smallest indivisible level causal chains are presumed to end."@en ;
    skos:note "Physics groundwork for the claim that the universe runs on probability, later used for the magical model."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 1 Quantum Weirdness, pp. 64-66; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSuperpositionAsHypertemporality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Superposition as hyper-temporality, entanglement as hyper-locality"@en, "Superposition als Hyper-Zeitlichkeit, Verschränkung als Hyper-Lokalität"@de ;
    skos:definition "Superposition and entanglement become intelligible if particles are taken as waves spreading into past and future of the observation; superposition implies simultaneous events, entanglement linked events at once in different places."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 1 Quantum Weirdness, p. 70; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTransactionalTimeReversal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Transactional interpretation and time reversibility"@en, "Transaktionale Interpretation und Zeitumkehrbarkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Following Cramer, a particle arises where a forward wave from the past meets a time-reversed wave from the future; time reversibility explains how a particle knows of two slits or of its entangled partner, but not randomness or superposition."@en ;
    skos:note "Cited approvingly as a step towards the author's own model."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 1 Quantum Weirdness, p. 71 (printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophThreeDimensionalTime a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Three-dimensional time"@en, "Dreidimensionale Zeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author proposes that time, like space, has three dimensions: besides fore-and-aft time a sideways time exists, in which probability runs perpendicular to clock time and acts as a pseudo-space of parallel universes; a universe so built would run on probability rather than strict causality, as this one is said to do."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, end of part 1 Quantum Weirdness and part 2 Three-dimensional time, pp. 72-73; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTemporalThicknessAsWave a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Temporal thickness as wave property"@en, "Zeitliche Dicke als Welleneigenschaft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Every object has a displacement in all three time dimensions; its temporal thickness at an instant is its wave property, room enough for superposed states; macroscopic bodies such as a human are nevertheless located fairly precisely and do not meaningfully exist in many parallel universes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 2 Three-dimensional time, p. 73; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSpaceTimeSymmetryArgument a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Space-time symmetry argument for 3D time"@en, "Raum-Zeit-Symmetrie als Argument für 3D-Zeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since time is measured only by motion in space and a deep symmetry links space and time, there is no reason to give them different dimensionality; wave aspects of matter move in several directions at once and occasionally show large effects as long-range entanglement or as the capricious phenomena of magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 2 Three-dimensional time, pp. 73-74; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPastFutureAsDiffuseWaves a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Past and future as diffuse wave modes"@en, "Vergangenheit und Zukunft als diffuse Wellenmoden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Seen from the present, past and future lack definite form; the present is the random collapse where waves from past and future meet; the past is only wave-like echoes of possibilities that never settled, so travel into it is called a silly idea, while travel into the future is possible only by near-light-speed time dilation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 2 Three-dimensional time, p. 75; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophModifyingTheProbablePast a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Modifying the probable past"@en, "Veränderung der wahrscheinlichen Vergangenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magic and quantum physics alike are said to permit altering the probable past, provided the present is not altered; magical anecdotes are said to suggest some enchantments act by changing what went before, and the delayed-choice eraser experiment is cited as demonstrating such an effect."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 2 Three-dimensional time, p. 75; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophUnexpectedFutureSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unexpected future as sign of a modified past"@en, "Unerwartete Zukunft als Zeichen veränderter Vergangenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "That the probable past has been modified is to be seen by the future then turning out in unexpected ways."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: the future turning out otherwise than expected shows that the probable past was altered. No deadline is set; the sign is observable only against a probable course that is itself never observed, so nearly any surprise qualifies; nobody is blamed when it does not come. The model is pmc:ChaosMagicApophModifyingTheProbablePast."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 2 Three-dimensional time, p. 75; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophGeneralMetadynamicsStatus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "General Metadynamics: status"@en, "Allgemeine Metadynamik: Status"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author calls his reading of quantum physics through reversible, three-dimensional time General Metadynamics; he admits that, as with rival interpretations, it cannot yet be falsified and is partly a matter of taste, though two lines of speculation (three particle generations; a vorticitating hyperspherical universe) are said to support it."@en ;
    skos:note "The author's own statement that the model cannot yet be falsified, entered as a statement on how fixed the doctrine is."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 2 Three-dimensional time, p. 76; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophThreeParticleGenerations a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Threefold particle families as support for 3D time"@en, "Dreifache Teilchenfamilien als Stütze der 3D-Zeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three generations of matter particles and charges occurring in threes are said to follow from the extra spin freedom of three time dimensions; reversible time accounts for antiparticles, also in threes (detailed in Appendix I)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 2 Three-dimensional time, p. 76; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophParaPhysics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Para-physics"@en, "Para-Physik"@de ;
    skos:definition "If divination and enchantment effects exist, they imply not mere psychology but a para-physics rooted in the quantum realm that juts erratically into everyday reality as magic; General Metadynamics plus decoherence is offered as its model."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 78; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophDecoherence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Decoherence"@en, "Dekohärenz"@de ;
    skos:definition "Superposed and entangled states are delicate and quickly get out of phase through contact with surrounding particles; this explains why the large-scale world is not ruled by quantum effects and why quantum computers need careful isolation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, pp. 78-79; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMagicWorksInPractice a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic works in practice, not yet in theory"@en, "Magie wirkt in der Praxis, noch nicht in der Theorie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magic is conceded to be unreliable even in practice, but parapsychological evidence on balance is said to show a limited real role, with divination and enchantment sometimes giving statistically impossible results; abandoning spirits and aethers leaves only quantum models."@en ;
    skos:note "The evidential claim beside the model; the model it leads to (the brain as a quantum computer of the chaotic analogue kind) is pmc:ChaosMagicCerebrumQuanticum."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 80; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophStateOfVoidness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "State of voidness"@en, "Zustand der Leere"@de ;
    skos:definition "A state in which parts of the brain apparently slacken into superposition, pregnant with possibilities from which inspiration can collapse; divinatory phenomena sometimes appear in it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the capacity; the model that places it in the brain is pmc:ChaosMagicCerebrumQuanticum."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 80; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophMindStillingMeditation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Mind-stilling meditation (or absent-minded distraction)"@en, "Geistberuhigende Meditation (oder zerstreute Ablenkung)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Still the mind in meditation; a milder form arises from absent-minded distraction."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 80; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMindStillingYieldsVoidness a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Mind-stilling yields voidness"@en, "Geistberuhigung ergibt Leere"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mind-stilling meditation or absent-minded distraction produces states of voidness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophMindStillingMeditation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophStateOfVoidness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 80; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophStateOfGnosisCoherence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "State of gnosis"@en, "Zustand der Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "A physiological state of the nervous system at the extreme of arousal or of concentration, said to correspond to coherence: large brain areas share one activity while others are strongly inhibited."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the capacity; the model that places it in the brain is pmc:ChaosMagicCerebrumQuanticum."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 81; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophGnosisByExcitationOrFocus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Gnosis by extreme excitation or extreme focus"@en, "Gnosis durch äußerste Erregung oder äußerste Fokussierung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Drive the nervous system into extreme excitation, or into extreme focus, until one activity dominates."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 81; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophExcitationYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Excitation/focus yields gnosis"@en, "Erregung/Fokus ergibt Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Extreme excitation or focus produces the gnostic state of coherence."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophGnosisByExcitationOrFocus ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophStateOfGnosisCoherence ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 81; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophClosedTimeLoopDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Closed time loop for divination"@en, "Geschlossene Zeitschleife für Divination"@de ;
    skos:definition "Visualise yourself at a future time when you will know the answer to the divination; resolve to visualise, once you have the answer, sending it back to your present divining self."@en ;
    skos:note "Reported as what some magicians do."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 81; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophClosedTimeLoopYieldsDivination a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Closed time loop yields divination"@en, "Zeitschleife ergibt Divination"@de ;
    skos:definition "The closed-loop visualisation is aimed at obtaining the divinatory answer."@en ;
    skos:note "The work reports the procedure of some magicians and states no result of it; the yield is the aim the procedure is built for."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophClosedTimeLoopDivination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDivinatioPerStatumFuturum ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 81; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophGnosisBeforeFutureMemory a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Future brain state needs visualisation with gnosis"@en, "Künftiger Gehirnzustand braucht Visualisierung mit Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "The future brain state on which enchantment rests is to be set up by visualisation joined to gnosis, so gnosis is part of what the future-memory spell requires."@en ;
    skos:note "Set as a technique to be used, not as a sequence the work makes binding; no consequence of leaving gnosis out is stated."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicIncantatioMemoriaFutura ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophGnosisByExcitationOrFocus ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 81; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophRetroactiveEnchantment a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Retroactive enchantment"@en, "Rückwirkende Verzauberung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Cast a statement of intent about a past age as accomplished fact, e.g. that at a given past age one had experiences that equipped one well for the future."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 82; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophRetroactiveEnchantmentYieldsRelief a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Retroactive enchantment yields undoing of past harm"@en, "Rückwirkende Verzauberung hebt vergangene Schäden auf"@de ;
    skos:definition "A spell cast as an accomplished fact about a past age is said possibly to undo many negative psychological and physical effects that seemed to stem from experiences at that age."@en ;
    skos:note "The capacity, with its limit (no change to the measurable present) and its only measure (deviation of the future from its probable course), is entered as pmc:ChaosMagicIncantatio."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophRetroactiveEnchantment ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicIncantatio ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 82; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMetadynamicUncertaintyRelation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Metadynamic uncertainty relation"@en, "Metadynamische Unschärferelation"@de ;
    skos:definition "General Metadynamics is given an equation of the Heisenberg type: indeterminacy of entropy (over temperature) times indeterminacy of time in any of its three dimensions is about Planck's constant; minimal-entropy activity can therefore last long, letting a particle feel out many futures of which only one is reinforced by reverse-time feedback."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 83; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophApophusisApophasisApoptosis a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Apophusis, Apophasis, Apoptosis"@en, "Apophusis, Apophasis, Apoptosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three-dimensional time is said to work by three stages: Apophusis (branching: reality feints at every option), Apophasis (weirdness: unrealised events still influence realised ones), Apoptosis (dying off, collapse of superposition and entanglement into a result); the mind subjectively seems to work likewise when seeking decision, inspiration or apophenia."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, pp. 83-84; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophQuantumPanpsychicPrinciple a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Quantum-panpsychic principle"@en, "Quanten-panpsychisches Prinzip"@de ;
    skos:definition "The likeness between the threefold process of reality and the felt working of the mind suggests some quantum-panpsychic principle operating in microcosm and macrocosm."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 3 General Metadynamics and magic, p. 84; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTimeAsRelativeChange a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Time as relative change; present radically unlike past and future"@en, "Zeit als relative Veränderung; Gegenwart grundlegend verschieden von Vergangenheit und Zukunft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Time is not something abstract apart from movement and change, does not flow and has no location; all records exist in the fleeting now; the difficulty of picturing time arises because past and future are of a radically different nature from the present (quanta caught only as particles, inferred to have behaved as waves)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 4 On The Nature of Time, pp. 84-85; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPresentAsInterferencePattern a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Present as interference pattern of a wave multiverse"@en, "Gegenwart als Interferenzmuster eines Wellen-Multiversums"@de ;
    skos:definition "The present always appears singular and particle-like; past and future are a vast array of waves, a multiverse of wave-like parallel worlds, from which the observed present arises as an interference pattern in a two-way process; this requires time to have width (three dimensions) to hold all possible pasts and futures."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 4 On The Nature of Time, pp. 85-86; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophBeingAsNecessaryIllusion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Being and a real past/future as necessary illusions"@en, "Sein und reale Vergangenheit/Zukunft als nötige Illusionen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Being is said to be an illusion of sluggish perception that misses ubiquitous change, and past and future are illusory since nothing particle-like is located there; we build this illusory picture because without it existence would probably be intolerable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 4 On The Nature of Time, p. 86; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMetadynamicsForMagicOnly a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "General Metadynamics as working paradigm for magic only"@en, "Allgemeine Metadynamik als Arbeitsparadigma nur für Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Taken to its conclusion General Metadynamics models the physics beneath both quantum physics and magic; as a Chaoist the author holds nothing true but things more or less useful, so he adopts it for magic as the most persuasive paradigm on offer while using less austere paradigms for ordinary life."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 4 On The Nature of Time, p. 86; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophDarkIlluminationCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Dark illumination of the metadynamic model"@en, "Dunkle Erleuchtung des metadynamischen Modells"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author warns that he finds the model an unpleasant insight, as troubling as the certainty of his own death, and mystically unattractive; hence he confines it to magical use."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophMetadynamicsForMagicOnly ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 5 Metadynamics - Practical Magic, part 4 On The Nature of Time, p. 86; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Chapter 6, Non-Singularity - Cosmology
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNonSingularityPrinciple a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Non-singularity principle"@en, "Prinzip der Nicht-Singularität"@de ;
    skos:definition "No real quantity can be infinite, though it may be finite and unbounded; singularities, a big bang start, a big crunch end or a creator god would make the universe rationally incomprehensible; the universe instead is finite and unbounded in space and time and exists simply because it is physically and magically self-consistent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 6 Non-Singularity - Cosmology, introduction, p. 88; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstBigBang a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against big bang cosmology"@en, "Gegen die Urknall-Kosmologie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author calls the big bang consensus erroneous: its evidence (redshift, microwave background, helium abundance, the last called circular) is reinterpreted, and its patches (inflation, dark matter, MOND, dark energy) are likened to phlogiston; cosmologists are said to defend it for their careers."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the author's side of a dispute with mainstream big bang cosmology; the other side was not read, and the dispute stands here only as his position."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 6 Non-Singularity - Cosmology, part 1 Against Singularity, pp. 88-92 (p. 91 printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophVorticitatingHypersphere a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vorticitating hyperspherical universe"@en, "Vortizitierende hypersphärische Welt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The universe is proposed as a gravitationally closed hypersphere (3-sphere) with small positive space-time curvature, whose volume slowly turns through its antipodes (vorticitation, about 22 billion years per turn), the centrifugal effect balancing gravity so it neither collapses nor expands; it has no centre, no edge and no outside."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 6 Non-Singularity - Cosmology, part 2 The Hyper-Spherical Universe, pp. 92-97; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophHypersphereExplainsCosmicData a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hypersphere explanations of cosmological data"@en, "Hypersphären-Erklärungen kosmologischer Daten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Pioneer (Anderson) deceleration is read as the curvature signature giving an antipode distance of about 11 billion light years; redshift then follows from distance, supernova mismatch from hyperspherical lensing, flat galactic rotation from vorticitation, helium abundance from stellar recycling equilibrium, and the microwave background from light circling the universe and thermalising with intergalactic gas."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 6 Non-Singularity - Cosmology, part 2 The Hyper-Spherical Universe, pp. 96-98; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophFiniteUnboundedTime a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Finite unbounded time, no eternal recurrence"@en, "Endliche unbegrenzte Zeit, keine ewige Wiederkehr"@de ;
    skos:definition "The three-dimensional time of chapter 5 applies to the whole universe, giving finite unbounded time with a temporal horizon of roughly 11 billion years, though things can persist longer; returning to the same spacetime point after a vorticitation does not mean the same events recur."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 6 Non-Singularity - Cosmology, part 2 The Hyper-Spherical Universe and part 3 Hyperspherical Metaphysics, pp. 97-99; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophExistenceFromSelfConsistency a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Existence from self-consistency; against creation from nothing"@en, "Existenz aus Selbstkonsistenz; gegen Schöpfung aus dem Nichts"@de ;
    skos:definition "One can answer why the universe exists (it is self-consistent) but not how it got that way; nothing is not more fundamental than something, nothing is seen to arise from nothing, and the idea of an origin from nothing is suspected to come from reifying being and privative notions like nothing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 6 Non-Singularity - Cosmology, part 3 Hyperspherical Metaphysics, p. 99; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophHypersphereNotTheoryOfEverything a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hypersphere model: incomplete, not a theory of everything"@en, "Hypersphärenmodell: unvollständig, keine Weltformel"@de ;
    skos:definition "The model is explicitly not a theory of everything (Gödel incompleteness is cited); it is called more elegant than the big bang, resting on four measured constants (G, c, h, curvature parameter) whose values it cannot derive; nothing is true, the most self-consistent hypotheses are most useful until superseded; chapter 7 tests whether psi supplies the missing ingredient."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 6 Non-Singularity - Cosmology, part 3 Hyperspherical Metaphysics, pp. 99-100; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMapsByJourney a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Maps by journey: from flat earth to hyperspherical spacetime"@en, "Karten nach Reise: von flacher Erde zur hypersphärischen Raumzeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "A sequence of world-maps, each adequate to a range of journey and each relocating gods and demons: flat earth (gods above, devils below), spherical earth (gods retreat to unseen dimensions), flat space (gods only in human hearts), curved spacetime (apocalyptic universe governed by blind chance or by a god who is away), vorticitating hyperspherical spacetime (no beginning or end, mind integral to its chance; a participatory universe whose geometry allows magic and invites humans to train as gods)."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 6 Non-Singularity - Cosmology, part 4 The Map, the Journey and the Meaning, pp. 100 and 103; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophChaoPanpsychicTreeOfLife a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chao-Panpsychic Tree of Life"@en, "Chao-panpsychischer Lebensbaum"@de ;
    skos:definition "Twelve levels: 0 fundamentals, 1 atoms, 2 unicellular life, 3 invertebrates, 4 vertebrates, 5 reptiles, 6 mammals and birds, 7 humans, 8 angels and gods (our mostly foolish dreams of improvement), 9 aliens, 10 unimaginably advanced life, 11 Psi / cosmic panpsychism; from level 7 shamanism and science look down, religion and mysticism up, magic in all directions; the Kabbalist may read it top-down, the scientist bottom-up."@en ;
    skos:note "Diagram page; its printed number is misread by OCR as 104 between pp. 100 and 102."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 6 Non-Singularity - Cosmology, part 4 The Map, the Journey and the Meaning, diagram pages, pp. 101-102 (p. 101 printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophLordCthulhuNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Lord Cthulhu on the Tree of Life"@en, "Lord Cthulhu im Lebensbaum"@de ;
    skos:definition "Cthulhu is placed as presiding over level 3 (invertebrates), where life gets nasty; a playful assignment within the diagram."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Lord Cthulhu" ;
    pm:nameRole "presiding figure of the invertebrate level" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 6 Non-Singularity - Cosmology, part 4 The Map, the Journey and the Meaning, diagram page, p. 102; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Chapter 7, Illumination!
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophFifthThermodynamicPrinciple a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Proposed extra law of thermodynamics"@en, "Vorgeschlagener zusätzlicher Hauptsatz der Thermodynamik"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second law is called a tautology (after Hawking, entropy increases because time is measured that way) and lacking cosmic applicability; a further law is entertained that energy-dissipating structures tend towards greater efficiency and complexity, as life seems to show, and gravitation and nuclear forces may recycle stellar ash into hydrogen."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 7 Illumination!, part 1 A Fifth Principle of Thermodynamics?, pp. 104-106; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMostSophisticatedIntelligencesExist a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Most sophisticated possible intelligences already exist"@en, "Die höchstmöglichen Intelligenzen existieren bereits"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since life can go round the temporal curvature without limit, if a complexity law holds then the most sophisticated intelligences the universe can support must already exist, probably in very large numbers."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter opens by announcing that it seeks support for the ontological argument, which it glosses as: what we can imagine as gods probably exists; this node and pmc:ChaosMagicApophLifeSelectsItsUniverse are where the support is given."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 7 Illumination!, part 2 What Can Have Evolved?, p. 106; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophScienceFictionGods a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Science fiction gods"@en, "Science-Fiction-Götter"@de ;
    skos:definition "Highly advanced extraterrestrial intelligences very probably exist; the author doubts they take much interest in us, suspects they generally avoid intervening (interference would spoil their interest) and at most act on rare whim; they likely want to watch what humans achieve unaided."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 7 Illumination!, part 3 Science Fiction Gods, pp. 107-109 (p. 107 number absent in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophJhvhNumberPlateNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "JHVH as an alien's number plate (satire)"@en, "JHVH als Kennzeichen eines Außerirdischen (Satire)"@de ;
    skos:definition "In a satirical dialogue between alien observers, an anthropologist who once scolded humans is said to have left them fixated on her vehicle's registration plate, reading JHIVH; the biblical god-name is thus playfully held to be a misread alien registration."@en ;
    skos:note "Fictional vignette in dialogue form, not asserted as fact. The layer spells the plate JHIVH; the intended form is presumably the tetragrammaton JHVH, which the layer may have misread."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "JHIVH" ;
    pm:nameRole "satirical origin of the Hebrew divine name" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 7 Illumination!, part 3, pp. 107-108 (p. 107 number absent in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstMultiverse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against multiverse hypotheses"@en, "Gegen Multiversum-Hypothesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fine-tuning of constants is granted and the anthropic principle called unarguable, but multiverse accounts (baby universes budding from black holes, cyclic crunch-and-bang, the vast solution spaces of string and brane theory) are said to violate Occam's razor badly and to leave unanswered where the mass-energy comes from and what the elsewhere means."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the author's side of a dispute with multiverse, string and brane theorists; the other side was not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 7 Illumination!, part 4 A Panpsychic Universe?, pp. 110-111; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophLifeSelectsItsUniverse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Life selects its own universe by retroactive causality"@en, "Leben wählt sein Universum durch rückwirkende Kausalität"@de ;
    skos:definition "Applying General Metadynamics to the proposed fifth principle of thermodynamics and adding panpsychism: there is a single universe, bound to contain life since retroactive causation in circular time lets life choose the conditions it needs; thus all mind-behaviour jointly is the God who fixes the universe's parameters, humans contributing more than atoms but far from omnipotent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 7 Illumination!, part 4 A Panpsychic Universe?, pp. 111-113; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophChaomerasNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "The Chaomeras: Pareidolia, Apophenia, Eris"@en, "Die Chaomeren: Pareidolia, Apophenia, Eris"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three Wyrd (Weird) Sisters of Chaos and guises of the Muse: Pareidolia reading augury from entrails, Apophenia seeking hidden connections, Eris disordering expectations; called challenging Muses."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Chaomeras; Three Wyrd Sisters of Chaos" ;
    pm:nameRole "muse-goddesses of chaotic inspiration" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 7 Illumination!, part 4 A Panpsychic Universe?, verse page, p. 112; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophFiveConstantsFiveElements a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Five constants as five elements"@en, "Fünf Konstanten als fünf Elemente"@de ;
    skos:definition "The constants G, c, h, the curvature parameter and Psi are set beside Water, Air, Earth, Fire and Spirit, said to fit the Law of Fives of Eris; a panpsychic collective universe is said to explain the diversity of its contents, including the unpleasant parts."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 7 Illumination!, part 4 A Panpsychic Universe?, p. 113; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Chapter 8, An Invocation of Apophenia
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophApopheniaMotherOfInventionNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Apophenia (goddess)"@en, "Apophenia (Göttin)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Personified principle of finding meanings and connections others have not, said to underlie both psychosis and genius and to be the real mother of invention; of Uranian quality, outside the seven classical planets, at the frontier of known knowledge; she merely unlocks the gates and enjoys whatever comes through; a god form existing only since 2005, needing support from her priests and priestesses and giving much in return."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Apophenia" ;
    pm:nameRole "goddess / god form of inspiration and connection-finding" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, parts 1-3 Introduction, General Observations and The General Form of the Invocation, pp. 114-118 (p. 114 printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPareidoliaSisterNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Pareidolia (goddess)"@en, "Pareidolia (Göttin)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sister of Apophenia who grants visions in apparent emptiness; her insights mostly feed art and religion; called crazy; welcomed in the invocation with a Rorschach blot."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Pareidolia" ;
    pm:nameRole "sister goddess of visions" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, parts 1-2 Introduction and General Observations, pp. 114 and 117; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophInducingTechniquesFromMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Apophenia-inducing techniques come mostly from magic"@en, "Apophenie-Techniken stammen meist aus der Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Most disciplines have no formal procedure for inducing apophenia and wait for intuition; art has tried stochastic methods (random paint fall, cut-up); most such methods derive from magic and occultism through their link with sortilege and divination."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 1 Introduction, p. 114; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophKabbalaFromApophenia a pm:Receiving ;
    rdfs:label "Apophenia herself on the origin of Kabbala"@en, "Apophenia selbst über den Ursprung der Kabbala"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that Kabbala originated as a method to induce apophenia is attributed parenthetically to the goddess herself as having told the author."@en ;
    pm:receivedBy "Peter J. Carroll, the author" ;
    pm:claimedSpeaker "Apophenia, as Herself" ;
    pm:receptionCircumstance "Stated only in a parenthesis after the sentence, that She told him so Herself; no state, occasion, date, questioner or record of the telling is given." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 1 Introduction, p. 114; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophApophenicInsight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Apophenia (inspired insight)"@en, "Apophenie (inspirierte Einsicht)"@de ;
    skos:definition "The faculty of finding new meaningful connections between existing ideas, yielding eccentric insights, problem solutions and invention in any discipline; helpful in magic and science."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, parts 1-2 Introduction and General Observations, pp. 114-117; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophGematriaForApophenia a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Gematria as apophenia technique"@en, "Gematrie als Apophenie-Technik"@de ;
    skos:definition "Give letters, words and phrases of a text number values, then seek other words or phrases with equal sums to draw out hidden meanings and inspiration."@en ;
    skos:note "Ascribed to the ancient Hebrew sages; the insights later ossified into divine maps."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 1 Introduction, pp. 114-115; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophRotatingDiscCombination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Rotating-disc concept combination (Zairja, Ars Magna)"@en, "Begriffskombination mit Drehscheiben (Zairja, Ars Magna)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Inscribe letters, words or symbols on stacked rotating discs of decreasing size and turn them to throw up random new pairings of concepts."@en ;
    skos:note "Ascribed to the Muslim Zairja and to Ramon Lull, taken up by Leibniz."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 1 Introduction, p. 115; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophGarbageInGarbageOutCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Garbage in, garbage out"@en, "Müll rein, Müll raus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Combination devices like Lull's yield only as good as what is loaded onto them; his were filled mainly with theological ideas."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicApophRotatingDiscCombination ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 1 Introduction, p. 115; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophCombinatoryTechniquesYieldApophenia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Combinatory techniques yield apophenia"@en, "Kombinatorische Techniken ergeben Apophenie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Gematria and rotating-disc combination are presented as techniques for inducing apophenia."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophGematriaForApophenia, pmp:ChaosMagicApophRotatingDiscCombination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophApophenicInsight ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 1 Introduction, pp. 114-116; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophDynamicIdeationalGeometry a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dynamic Ideational Geometry"@en, "Dynamische Ideengeometrie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The random combination of graphically represented concepts and their geometric relations, called a tremendously powerful tool for inducing apophenia in nearly any discipline and the basis of the invocation that follows."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 1 Introduction, p. 116; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophMildInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Mild invocation of Apophenia"@en, "Milde Anrufung der Apophenia"@de ;
    skos:definition "For particular problems: some work at a desk on the subject, perhaps followed by a woodland walk."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 2 General Observations, p. 116; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophIntenseInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Intense invocation of Apophenia"@en, "Intensive Anrufung der Apophenia"@de ;
    skos:definition "An elaborate ritual with cut-up incantations, disinhibiting or hallucinatory sacraments, intense contemplation of odd glyphs and diagrams, and sleep deliberately disrupted; in such workings the magician may conceive of himself as Apophenia in person."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 2 General Observations, p. 116; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophIntenseInvocationAftereffectsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "After-effects of intense invocation"@en, "Nachwirkungen intensiver Anrufung"@de ;
    skos:definition "An intense working may leave the operator mentally hyperactive, somewhat disturbed and possibly pareidolic; a formal banishing may follow, best followed in turn by deliberate return to mundane activity, especially physical work."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicApophIntenseInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 2 General Observations, p. 116; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophBanishingAndMundaneReimmersion a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Banishing followed by mundane re-immersion"@en, "Bannung mit anschließender Rückkehr ins Alltägliche"@de ;
    skos:definition "After an intense working perform a formal banishing, then deliberately plunge into ordinary activity, above all physical labour."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 2 General Observations, p. 116; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophInvocationYieldsApophenia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Invocation yields apophenia"@en, "Anrufung ergibt Apophenie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The mild, intense and full invocations aim at and are said to give apophenic inspiration on any chosen subject at any desired intensity."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophMildInvocation, pmp:ChaosMagicApophIntenseInvocation, pmp:ChaosMagicApophFullInvocationOfApophenia, pmp:ChaosMagicApophGroupTableWork ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophApophenicInsight ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 2 General Observations, p. 116; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAgainstPlatoNecessity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against Plato: necessity is not the mother of invention"@en, "Gegen Platon: Not ist nicht die Mutter der Erfindung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author says Plato was wrong to make necessity the mother of invention; inventions rarely come to order but from finding inspiring connections between existing ideas, so apophenia is the true mother of invention."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the author's side of a dispute with Plato; Plato was not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 2 General Observations, pp. 116-117; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophOuranosGateBreakerNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ouranos (macho god form)"@en, "Ouranos (machohafte Gottform)"@de ;
    skos:definition "A more macho Uranian god form that tries to break open the gates of the unknown by antinomian conjuration and to impose form on what it finds; Apophenia is offered as a complementary entry to Uranian magic."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Ouranos" ;
    pm:nameRole "Uranian god form" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 2 General Observations, p. 117; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophErisNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Eris (goddess of discord)"@en, "Eris (Göttin der Zwietracht)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Goddess of discord who may appear beyond the gates to toss paradox and muddle into the work just to stir things up; welcomed in the invocation by looking at her sign while pondering the collision of opposites; patron of the Law of Fives."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Eris" ;
    pm:nameRole "goddess of discord, third Weird Sister" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 2 General Observations and part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, pp. 117-119; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophWeirdSistersCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Beware the Weird Sisters of Chaos"@en, "Hütet euch vor den Schicksalsschwestern des Chaos"@de ;
    skos:definition "Beyond the gates Pareidolia or Eris may appear in Apophenia's place; the three sisters make challenging muses."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicApophFullInvocationOfApophenia ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 2 General Observations, p. 117; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSymbolOfApophenia a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Symbol of Apophenia"@en, "Symbol der Apophenia"@de ;
    skos:definition "Five elements, a cross, a circle and three crescent moons, merging Uranian, solar, lunar and Venusian currents with a trace of Mercury."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 2 General Observations, p. 117; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPreparationBeforeInvocation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Lengthy preparation before the invocation"@en, "Lange Vorbereitung vor der Anrufung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Considerable time and effort must go into preparing materials and concepts before the invocation, because imagination and belief take time to build to useful levels (as the old grimoires knew)."@en ;
    skos:note "A reason is given but no explicit consequence of skipping."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophFullInvocationOfApophenia ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophPreparingInvocationInstruments ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, pp. 117-118; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophCocoaSacrament a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Acclimatising to the sacrament"@en, "Gewöhnung an das Sakrament"@de ;
    skos:definition "During the preparation period practise consuming the sacrament to get used to its taste and effects. The sacrament is a very strong hot-water decoction of cocoa, its theobromine being the active basis; confectionery chocolate is unsuitable, being mostly fat and sugar."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, pp. 118 and 120; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSacramentAcclimatisationFirst a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Preparation acclimatisation before sacrament use"@en, "Vorherige Gewöhnung vor Sakramentsgebrauch"@de ;
    skos:definition "Practice with the sacrament is suggested during preparation before its use in the rite."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophFullInvocationOfApophenia ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophCocoaSacrament ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, p. 118; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophGroupTableWork a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Group table work"@en, "Tabellenarbeit in der Gruppe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Usually done alone, but the table work can become rapid-fire brainstorming by word association among several operators at an altar or desk."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, p. 118; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophPreparingInvocationInstruments a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Preparing the Apophenia instruments"@en, "Herstellung der Apophenia-Instrumente"@de ;
    skos:definition "In advance make: a wand a little longer than a hand, of any material (cut from thick aluminium sheet recommended for its symbolism); card or stiff-paper tables of twofold, threefold, fourfold and fivefold figures; an Astronomicon (a matt black disc at least a hand across, e.g. enamelled steel, with movable coloured, e.g. magnetic, discs for Uranus and the seven classical planets); a symbol of Eris and materials for a Rorschach blot, placed at the ends of the altar; writing instruments for additions and insights."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, pp. 118-119; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophFullInvocationOfApophenia a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Full invocation of Apophenia"@en, "Vollständige Anrufung der Apophenia"@de ;
    skos:definition "(i) Optional banishing (Gnostic Pentagram Ritual suggested); (ii) light incense of agreeable and disagreeable ingredients (oakmoss and valerian root) and take the sacrament if desired; (1) declare the intent; (2) spontaneous vernacular appeal to Apophenia, spoken or mental; (3) with the wand draw her symbol in air or smoke and visualise inhaling it to fill the whole body, with fast breathing to over-oxygenate the brain and optional erotic or autoerotic excitation as further gnosis; (4) recite the memorised 23-word Ouranian-Barbaric invocation; (5) as Apophenia welcome Eris by gazing at her symbol and briefly contemplating the clash of opposites, welcome Pareidolia by making and contemplating a Rorschach blot, and politely ask both to stay at the edge of the rite; (6) work the prepared tables, pointing the wand at figures that catch attention and using the moon horns as bridges between concepts, chanting the invocation at will, and at random or inspired moments move pieces on the Astronomicon to contemplate planetary conjunctions; continue until exhaustion or inspiration; (7) banish only if needed. Improvise and amend on inspiration."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, pp. 119-121 (p. 119 printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophMemoriseInvocationFirst a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Memorise the invocation before the rite"@en, "Anrufung vor dem Ritus auswendig lernen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The invocation is to be committed to memory beforehand."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophFullInvocationOfApophenia ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicApophLearningBarbaricInvocation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, p. 119; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophBlockedStream a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Blocked stream of consciousness"@en, "Blockierter Bewusstseinsstrom"@de ;
    skos:definition "A stream of association during table work can become blocked; the Astronomicon is then used to start another stream or as distraction."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the stream of association in the table work stalls. It is met inside the rite, by turning to the Astronomicon for a fresh stream or a distraction, and does not count against the rite; no deadline; observable only to the operator; nobody is blamed. Concerns pmp:ChaosMagicApophFullInvocationOfApophenia."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, p. 120; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophInspirationAfterExhaustion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Inspiration after exhaustion"@en, "Inspiration nach Erschöpfung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Work goes on until exhaustion or inspiration arrives; inspiration may rush into the emptiness of the relaxation after exhaustion."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: inspiration arrives, possibly only in the relaxation after the work has run to exhaustion. The session itself is the only time limit; whether inspiration came is judged by the operator alone; the work does not say what follows if neither inspiration nor anything else comes. Concerns pmp:ChaosMagicApophFullInvocationOfApophenia."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, p. 120; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPersistingPhenomenaCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Persisting disagreeable phenomena"@en, "Anhaltende unangenehme Erscheinungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A final banishing should be used only if disagreeable phenomena persist after the work."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicApophFullInvocationOfApophenia ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, part 3 The General Form of the Invocation, p. 120; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophLearningBarbaricInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Learning the Ouranian-Barbaric invocation"@en, "Erlernen der ouranisch-barbarischen Anrufung"@de ;
    skos:definition "To maximise efficacy, memorise the 23 barbarous words by repeated chanting until fluent while avoiding consciously learning the English meaning; read the vernacular version a few times, then obliterate it from the page."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, The Apophenia Invocation, p. 122 (number absent in layer; between pp. 121 and 123); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophNeitherNeitherDualities a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Neither-Neither on dualities"@en, "Weder-Noch an Dualitäten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Take a duality; consider first one side alone, then the other, then both joined, then the simultaneous absence of both, and see what arises."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, A Table of Dualities, p. 123 (printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTableOfDualities a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Table of Dualities"@en, "Tafel der Dualitäten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Example starting pairs, to be extended at will (here regrouped): physical (mass/energy, space/time, causality/randomness); psychological (fear/desire, love/hate, will/perception, sex/death); mystical (ego/enlightenment, atman/anatta, being/doing, Choronzon/Baphomet, eristic/aneristic delusion); cultural (art/religion, magic/science)."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, A Table of Dualities, p. 123 (printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophTrialityMeditation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Triality meditation"@en, "Trialitäten-Meditation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Concentrate on the concept at each corner of a selected triangle one after another, then consider how they may give rise to one another in clockwise or anticlockwise order."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, A Table of Trialities, p. 124; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTableOfTrialities a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Table of Trialities"@en, "Tafel der Trialitäten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Example triangles (diagram, OCR-damaged): order-disorder with a third term; materialism-magic-transcendentalism (trance of the aeons); Apophenia-Eris-Pareidolia (Weird Sisters of Chaos); self-universe-others."@en ;
    skos:note "Diagram text largely garbled in the layer; vertices partly reconstructed and uncertain."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, p. 124; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophQuadradConstruction a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Quadrad construction"@en, "Bildung von Quadraden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Cross two dualities as the axes of a graph and try to give each of the four fields a meaning."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, A Table of Quadrads, p. 125 (printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTableOfQuadrads a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Table of Quadrads"@en, "Tafel der Quadraden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Examples: known/unknown crossed with knowns/unknowns (Rumsfeld's paradox); good/evil by law/chaos (game character generator); air/fire/water/earth (traditional alchemical); provable/unprovable by useful/meaningless (concept evaluator); sex/food/death and a fourth (quadrad of the appetites)."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, A Table of Quadrads, p. 125 (printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophIronLawOfFives a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Iron Law of Fives"@en, "Ehernes Gesetz der Fünf"@de ;
    skos:definition "All things are said (half in jest) to obey the Law of Fives: any number can be got from five, and five is the divine spark or awkward extra beyond foursquare ordinariness, hence its appeal to magicians and antinomians; wherever four appears, look for a fifth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, A Table of Pentads, p. 126; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTableOfPentads a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Table of Pentads"@en, "Tafel der Pentaden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Examples: spirit over air, fire, water, earth (advanced alchemy); illumination over invocation, enchantment, divination, evocation (magical operations)."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Peter J. Carroll, in The Apophenion" ;
    dcterms:date "2008"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, A Table of Pentads, p. 126; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApophFindingPentads a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Finding pentads"@en, "Pentaden finden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Wherever a fourfold scheme appears, look for its completing fifth; meditate on what could sit on an axis piercing a quadrad's plane."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, A Table of Pentads, p. 126; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTableExercisesYieldApophenia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Table techniques yield apophenia"@en, "Tafeltechniken ergeben Apophenie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Neither-neither, triality, quadrad and pentad work are the table exercises of the invocation meant to produce apophenic insight."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApophNeitherNeitherDualities, pmp:ChaosMagicApophTrialityMeditation, pmp:ChaosMagicApophQuadradConstruction, pmp:ChaosMagicApophFindingPentads ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicApophApophenicInsight ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), ch. 8 An Invocation of Apophenia, Tables of Dualities, Trialities, Quadrads and Pentads, pp. 123-126; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Appendix I, Three-dimensional time and quantum geometry
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophHyperspinEightDimensional a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Geometricate the quanta (HD8 hypothesis)"@en, "Die Quanten geometrisieren (HD8-Hypothese)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since quantum gravity has failed, the author proposes geometricating quanta: in Hyperspin Eight Dimensional (HD8) particles are quanta of spacetime with spin, their properties arising from rotations of three space and three time axes about the two curvature axes (the fourth of space and of time); the spin classes give chirality, colour, electroweak and generational charges, and a few rules (incl. t4-axis neutrality) are said to reproduce the whole observed particle suite; no mass algorithm yet. It is further said to account for the seeming failure of generation to be conserved when particles interact and for the parity violation shown by neutrinos and W bosons."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix I Three-dimensional time and quantum geometry, parts 1-2 The Prologue to a Quantum Geometry and Fundamental particles in eight dimensions, pp. 127-132 (p. 127 printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophHd8Predictions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "HD8 predictions: neutron annihilation, no black holes, no Higgs"@en, "HD8-Vorhersagen: Neutronenannihilation, keine Schwarzen Löcher, kein Higgs"@de ;
    skos:definition "HD8 is said to predict that neutrinos (and at high energy neutrons) annihilate in head-on collision, so galactic cores form neutron stars that shed matter rather than black holes or singularities, and that there is no Higgs boson, mass being intrinsic to the particles' fourth-dimensional nature."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix I Three-dimensional time and quantum geometry, part 2, p. 132; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophStaticFieldsAsCurvature a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Static fields as instantaneous curvature"@en, "Statische Felder als instantane Krümmung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author suspects static fields are spacetime curvatures propagating instantaneously without virtual bosons; he concedes this is hard if not impossible to falsify and seems to contradict special relativity, though unusable for signalling; gravitons would be neutrino-antineutrino-type bosons from cataclysmic accelerations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix I Three-dimensional time and quantum geometry, part 2, pp. 132-133; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophStrangeQuarksAndMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Strange quarks and magic share a cause"@en, "Strange-Quarks und Magie haben eine Ursache"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three-dimensional time is said to explain particle physics besides magic and quantum oddity: strange quarks and magic both occasionally feature in reality because reality has three-dimensional time; pi as irrational ratio of each dimension to its curvature may supply the chaotic basis of random quantum collapse."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix I Three-dimensional time and quantum geometry, part 3 Summary, p. 134; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Appendix II, Hypersphere from Radius Excess
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophRadiusExcessHypersphere a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Radius excess forces hyperspheres, not singularities"@en, "Radiusüberschuss erzwingt Hypersphären statt Singularitäten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Using Schwarzschild's radius excess, a sufficiently dense sphere must become a hypersphere once its diameter reaches half its circumference; hence spacetime singularities are unlikely either at the start of the universe or from collapse."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix II Hypersphere from Radius Excess, pp. 135-137 (p. 135 printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Appendix III, The Hyperspherical Universe
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophSimpleAlgebraHunch a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hunch of simple fundamental algebra"@en, "Ahnung einfacher Grundalgebra"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author states a hunch that basic algebra and geometry govern the cosmos and that highly elaborate equations have no place at the fundamental level."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix III The Hyperspherical Universe, p. 139; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophHypersphereSizeFromAnderson a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vorticitating hypersphere from Gödel's rotation"@en, "Vortizitierende Hypersphäre aus Gödels Rotation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Starting from Gödel's rotating solution, giving a hypersphere an orbital velocity of lightspeed yields a vorticitation of its whole 3-surface about the curvature axis whose centrifugal acceleration balances gravity; equating that acceleration with the measured Anderson acceleration gives universe mass, an antipode distance and temporal horizon of about 11 billion (light) years, with about 15% uncertainty."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix III The Hyperspherical Universe, parts 1-2 The Vorticitating Hypersphere and The Size of the Universe, pp. 139-141; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophClosedTimeCurvesCausality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Closed time curves make everything the cause of everything"@en, "Geschlossene Zeitkurven machen alles zur Ursache von allem"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Anderson effect is said to give an omnidirectional resistance to motion and a boost to orbital velocities that removes the need for dark matter; the closed time curves of a vorticitating hypersphere, which made Gödel's model look unphysical, are said not to create a causality problem but to solve it since all things become causes of all things, though nothing survives the 22-billion-year circuit unchanged. The other objection that sank Gödel's rotating model, that no rotation axis is seen, is met by placing the axis at right angles to all three dimensions of space, where it shows only as curvature."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix III The Hyperspherical Universe, parts 3-4 The Anderson Acceleration and Closed Time Curves, pp. 141-142 (p. 141 printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophParticlesAsHyperspheres a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Particles as vorticitating hyperspheres: microcosm equals macrocosm"@en, "Teilchen als vortizitierende Hypersphären: Mikrokosmos gleich Makrokosmos"@de ;
    skos:definition "The hypersphere rotation equation, read with antipode distance as wavelength, is said to give the basic fermion spin unit and explain the 720-degree rotation of fermions; so fundamental particles are themselves vorticitating hyperspheres and one equation unites microcosm and macrocosm, which Hermes Trismegistus and the Sufis (everything spins) would supposedly appreciate."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix III The Hyperspherical Universe, part 5 Hyperspherical Particles, pp. 142-143; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Appendix IV, The Shape of the Universe
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophHypersphericalLensing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hyperspherical lensing mimics accelerating expansion"@en, "Hypersphärische Linsenwirkung täuscht beschleunigte Expansion vor"@de ;
    skos:definition "Assuming flat space in a hyperspherical universe causes horizon problems like a flat-earth belief: geodesics act as a giant lens magnifying objects near half the antipode distance and shrinking farther ones, so redshift read as expansion plus magnitude mismatches leads observers wrongly to infer an expanding and accelerating universe of what is really constant size."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix IV The Shape of the Universe, pp. 145-147 (p. 147 printed number misread in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Appendix V, Apophenia's Birthday
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophTheosynthesis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Theosynthesis: mages name new gods"@en, "Theosynthese: Magier benennen neue Götter"@de ;
    skos:definition "A mage can give the world a newly named god; ancient mages realised god forms for the basic impulses and for cities and tribes, and complex times need further gods to complete the occult pantheon; each such god is said to have stalked its priest through life like a shadow-genie until it identified itself and phenomenised."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix V Apophenia's Birthday, part 1 Theosynthesis and syncretism, pp. 148-149; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophHolyGuardianAngelNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Holy Guardian Angel (Mathers)"@en, "Heiliger Schutzengel (Mathers)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Given by Samuel MacGregor Mathers (Deo Duce) in his grimoire from fragments attributed to Abramelin; held to express his ultimate grasp of the principle of Higher Self and Secret Chiefs."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Holy Guardian Angel" ;
    pm:nameRole "god form of the Higher Self" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix V Apophenia's Birthday, part 1 Theosynthesis and syncretism, p. 148; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAiwassHorusTrueWillNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Aiwass-Horus-True-Will (Crowley)"@en, "Aiwass-Horus-Wahrer-Wille (Crowley)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Given by Aleister Crowley (Therion) in the Book of the Law; held to express his ultimate grasp of the inner Beast, glorious and horrible, beneath the veneer of civilisation."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Aiwass-Horus-True-Will" ;
    pm:nameRole "god form of the Beast Within" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix V Apophenia's Birthday, part 1 Theosynthesis and syncretism, p. 148; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophKiaNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Kia (Spare)"@en, "Kia (Spare)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Attributed (hedged: many think so, as he wrote in riddles) to Austin Spare (Zos); held to sum up his understanding of the panpsychism underlying phenomena, the all-devouring chaotic vitality underneath self-image."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Kia" ;
    pm:nameRole "all-devouring chaotic vitality under the self-image" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix V Apophenia's Birthday, part 1 Theosynthesis and syncretism, p. 148; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophApopheniaNativityNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Apophenia: nativity and nature"@en, "Apophenia: Geburt und Wesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Offered by the author (Stokastikos) as goddess embodying the occult style of thought that seeks hidden connections and the meanings they bring; the word is credited to Klaus Conrad (1958); she is said to have become manifest as a deity online on 26 May 2005, during an online exchange among Corlian, Stokastikos and Dalryada in a Maybelogic Academy chaos magic course, making her a Gemini with Pareidolia as her twin; the author says she has always had him."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Apophenia" ;
    pm:nameRole "goddess of the occult style of thought" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix V Apophenia's Birthday, parts 1-2 Theosynthesis and syncretism and A Surprise Birthday, pp. 149-152; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophNoDrivingCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Apophenia makes driving unsafe for the author"@en, "Apophenie macht dem Autor das Autofahren unsicher"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author reports that he refuses to drive motor vehicles because passing scenery sets off too many side-trains of thought and mild hallucinations to be safe at speed; offered as part of his account of a lifelong apophenic disposition."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-report about the trait the book invokes, not about a rite."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophApophenicInsight ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix V Apophenia's Birthday, part 1 Theosynthesis and syncretism, p. 149; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophEcologyOfIdeas a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Need for an ecology of ideas"@en, "Bedarf an einer Ökologie der Ideen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ideas and the problems they cause grow exponentially while knowledge fragments; humanity needs a holistic ecology of ideas and therefore synthesists, polymaths and visionaries, apopheniacs of all kinds, which is why the author commends Apophenia to magicians."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix V Apophenia's Birthday, part 1 Theosynthesis and syncretism, p. 150; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophPareidoliaTwinNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Pareidolia as Apophenia's twin"@en, "Pareidolia als Zwilling der Apophenia"@de ;
    skos:definition "Pareidolia shares Apophenia's birthday as her twin; the author first dismissed her as mere distraction (faces in random patterns), but chaos theologians stressed her importance for art and mystical religion; Dali and the author of Revelation are called her high priests; her formal invocations are left to magician-artists and mystics."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Pareidolia" ;
    pm:nameRole "twin goddess of visions, patron of art and mystical religion" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Appendix V Apophenia's Birthday, part 2 A Surprise Birthday, p. 152; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .


#
# Epilogue, untitled closing section
#

pmc:ChaosMagicApophParadigmSummary a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Summary of the chaos magic paradigm"@en, "Zusammenfassung des Chaosmagie-Paradigmas"@de ;
    skos:definition "Distilling a decade of research, the author holds that the universe is a vorticitating hypersphere of quantum-panpsychic character, finite and unbounded in space and time with equal dimensionality; its three-dimensional time explains quantum strangeness and occasional magic; random quantum statistics fake macroscopic causality while magic structures the universe, in which we take part unwittingly and can take part deliberately; deliberate magic is said to succeed frequently enough to be worth doing; multiple minds make neopantheist mythos-belief truer to the psyche than logos-belief; retroactive panpsychism may explain this participatory universe."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Epilogue (untitled closing section), p. 153 and the following page (number absent in layer); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApophAuthorsOwnApopheniaCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The author's own apophenia"@en, "Die eigene Apophenie des Autors"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author cautions that he is himself apophenic and others may view matters otherwise, there being many alternative realities around."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicApophParadigmSummary ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition), Epilogue (untitled closing section), second page (number absent in layer, after p. 153); read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .
